{"id": "ych1_v1_u0_c0_pdf009_0039", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": null, "section": "ABOUTTHIS BOOK", "content_type": "front_matter", "book_pages": null, "pdf_pages": [9, 9], "char_count": 879, "text": "ABOUTTHIS BOOK\n\nThisbookiswrittenforstudentsfollowingthePearsonEdexcel InternationalAdvancedSubsidiary(IAS) Chemistryspecification.ThisbookcoversthefullIAScourseandthefirstyearoftheInternational A Level (IAL) course.\n\nThebookcontainsfullcoverageofIASunits(orexampapers)1and 2.Studentscanprepareforthewritten PracticalPaperbyusingtheIALChemistryLabBook(seepagexof thisbook).EachunithasfiveTopic areasthatmatchthetitlesandorderofthoseinthespecification.YoucanrefertotheAssessmentOverview onpagexii forfurtherinformation.\n\nEachTopicisdividedintochaptersandsectionstobreakthecontentdownintomanageablechunks.Each sectionfeaturesamixoflearningandactivities.\n\nLEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\nEachchapterstartswith a list of key assessmentobjectives.\n\n1C\n\nCHECKPOINT\n\nTheexactspecification pointscovered inthe sectionareprovided.\n\n1COMPARINGMASSESOF SUBSTANCES\n\nSPECIFICATION REFERENCE\n\n1.11.2\n\n1.4"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u0_c0_pdf009_0043", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": null, "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "front_matter", "book_pages": null, "pdf_pages": [9, 9], "char_count": 417, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Uderstan t:rtv atomc ssdntCalelatlelass retive ms molar mass, as the mass per mole of a substance in g mol-1.\n\n• Understand how to calculate relative molecular mass and relative formula mass from relative atomic masses.\n\n• Perform calculations using the Avogadro constant L (6.02 x 10²3 mol1) ,\n\nRELATIVE ATOMIC MASS (A)\n\nNote that A, and M, d nt he unis Here are some examples of calculations."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u0_c0_pdf009_0045", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": null, "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1", "content_type": "front_matter", "book_pages": null, "pdf_pages": [9, 9], "char_count": 530, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1\n\nAs chemists discovered more and more elements in the nineteenth century, they began to realise that the masses of the elements were different They could not weigh ndi@ual atoms,but they were able to use mumbers to compare the marses of atoms of different elements For this resson, they began to ue the tem 'relative atomic mass', What is therelmlecurmass of candioxdeCO？ M, - 12.0 +(2×16.0)= 44.0\n\nWORKEDEXAMPLE2\n\nWhat is the relamive molecuiar mass of sulluric acid, H SO? M,= (2 × 7.0) - 32.1 + (4 x 16.0) = 98.1"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u0_c0_pdf009_0047", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": null, "section": "RELATIVE FORMULA MASS (M)", "content_type": "front_matter", "book_pages": null, "pdf_pages": [9, 9], "char_count": 658, "text": "RELATIVE FORMULA MASS (M)\n\nThis term has the same symbol as relative molcular mass, but the formula' pert means that it incldes both mojeculkes and ions. Wiorked example 3 below is slighrly more compicated because of the water f crystallisation, but there is also another problem. Hydrated copper(il) sulfase is an iccic compound, so it is not a good ide rfrtitsrelatve mmassTht is tyi called relative jbrmfe mass.\n\nWORKEDEXAMPLE3\n\nC.SO,5HO?\n\nWhat isth foss yratd s\n\nM, = 63.5 + 32.1 + [4 × 16.0) • 5[2 × 1.0) + 16.0] = 249.6\n\nThe term relacive formula mass'should aiso be used fo compounds with gint scructures, such as sodium chloride and sillicon dioxide."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u0_c0_pdf009_0049", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": null, "section": "MOLAR MASS (M)", "content_type": "front_matter", "book_pages": null, "pdf_pages": [9, 9], "char_count": 894, "text": "MOLAR MASS (M)\n\nAnother way around the problem in Worked exampie 3 is to\n\nunits of grams.\n\nAfter the discvery of isoopes the C isotope of arbon as used in the defniticn of relative atomic mas.\n\nThe chemists soon realised thatthe element whose atoms had the smallest.mass was hydrogm, so the relatve atomic mass of hydrogen was fxed s 1. Atoms of silcon had double the mass of nitrogen atoms, and nitrogen atoms were 14 times heavrler nitgenws 14, and thar of slln ws 28. At frt,msty whoe mumb were d bt ll it waspble tod tmss of an atom to sereral decimal pleoes The Periodic Table in the Data Booklet uses 1 decimal place for lighter elements and whole numbers for heavier ones.\n\nA suitable definition of relstive atomic mass ist:\n\nthe weighted mean (avernge) mass of an stom compared to of the mass of an atom of 1C It is ofen usedal to emember this expression:\n\nof the mass of an atom of 13C"}
