{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf015_0099", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "TOPIC1FORMULAE EQUATIONSANDAMOUNT OFSUBSTANCE", "content_type": "topic_divider", "book_pages": [2, 2], "pdf_pages": [15, 15], "char_count": 1321, "text": "TOPIC1FORMULAE EQUATIONSANDAMOUNT OFSUBSTANCE\n\nAATOMS,ELEMENTSANDMOLECULESIBEQUATIONS AND REACTIONTYPESIC ENERGYID EMPIRICAL ANDMOLECULARFORMULAEIECALCULATIONSWITH SOLUTIONSAND GASES\n\nStudyingchemistry atthislevel involvesmanyskills.Theseskillsincludedescribingobservations,giving explanations,planningandinterpretingexperiments.Althoughalloftheseskillsarediscussedinthistopic,the mostimportantfeatureofthispartof thecourseinvolvesusingnumbers.Somestudentshaveanaturalability for numbers, while others find them less attractive. Whatever your mathematical ability, it is important to spend asmuchtimeaspossiblefocusingon thistopicinordertoimprove.\n\nUsingnumbers accuratelyisanimportantskill insomany occupations-herearejust a fewexamples:\n\n• inmedicine,calculating the dosageofa drug to maximisebenefit to the patientandminimise unwanted side-effects\n\n• in business, predicting whether capital investment will produce a sufficient income to justify the investment\n\n• inindustry,consideringwhetherthecostsandpollutingeffectsofanewmethodofmakingamolecule justify making the change\n\n• in shippingoperations(for example,theCosta Concordiain2012),workingout thevolume ofairneeded to floatashipafteranaccident.\n\nAsfarasyourchemistrycourseisconcerned,thistopicisthefoundationofsuccessinothertopics,suchas energetics andkinetics."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf015_0100", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "MATHS SKILLS FOR THIS TOPIC", "content_type": "topic_divider", "book_pages": [2, 2], "pdf_pages": [15, 15], "char_count": 301, "text": "MATHS SKILLS FOR THIS TOPIC\n\n• Useappropriateunitsandconversions,includingformasses,gasandsolutionvolumes\n\n• Use standard and ordinaryform,decimal places,andsignificantfigures\n\n• Useratios,fractionsandpercentages,includinginyield and atomeconomycalculations\n\n• ·Rearrangeexpressionsandsubstitutevalues"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf016_0101", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "Whatpriorknowledge doI need?", "content_type": "topic_divider", "book_pages": [3, 3], "pdf_pages": [16, 16], "char_count": 396, "text": "Whatpriorknowledge doI need?\n\n• Using appropriate apparatus to measure masses andvolumes,andrecordingvaluestothe appropriateprecision\n\n• Knowinghowtoconvertbetweendifferentunits ofmassandvolume\n\n• Writing andbalancingchemical equations, includingthe useofstatesymbols\n\n• Usingthemoleastheunitforamountofsubstance\n\n• Calculatingtherelativeformulamassofa compoundfromrelativeatomicmassesof elements"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf016_0102", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "Whatwill I study in this topic?", "content_type": "topic_divider", "book_pages": [3, 3], "pdf_pages": [16, 16], "char_count": 360, "text": "Whatwill I study in this topic?\n\n• Usingthemoleinnewsituations,tocalculate masses,volumes,concentrationsandformulae\n\n• Using experiments to obtain evidence for chemical formulaeandequations\n\n• Calculating percentage yields and atom economies fromequations\n\n• Interpretingobservationsfromtest-tubereactions, includingdisplacement,neutralisationand precipitation"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf017_0103", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [4, 4], "pdf_pages": [17, 17], "char_count": 70, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Knowthetermsatom,element,ion,molecule compound."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf017_0104", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "THEPURPOSEOFTHISSECTION", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [4, 4], "pdf_pages": [17, 17], "char_count": 282, "text": "THEPURPOSEOFTHISSECTION\n\nIn your previous study of chemistry, you have learned about several terms at anintroductorylevel. In this book,you will study them ingreaterdepth.\n\nThis topic is designed to revise some of the terms that you have metbefore,andwill meet again in more detail."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf017_0105", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "WHATISANELEMENT?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [4, 4], "pdf_pages": [17, 17], "char_count": 1354, "text": "WHATISANELEMENT?\n\nWe can start by looking at a simplified version of thePeriodic Table ofElements.You have alreadylearned something about thePeriodicTableandwill learnmoreaboutit inTopic2.\n\nEachboxinthePeriodicTable contains a capital letter,ormore often apair of letters.Thefirst one is always acapital letter and thesecond oneisneveracapital letterEachletterorpair of lettersrepresentsanelement.Youwill knowsomeofthemfrom your everyday life.For example,oxygen is an element youbreathe, andiron isanelementusedtomakebodiesofcars andbicycles.\n\nYoumayhavebeentaughtthatanelementisasubstancethat containsatoms ofonlyone type.Elementsare chemically the simplest substances so they cannot be broken down using chemical reactions.For example,neon is an element because it contains only neon atoms,which havethe symbol Ne.It cannot bebroken down into atomsof any otherelement.Wateris not anelementbecauseitcanbebrokendowninto theelements hydrogen and oxygen.\n\nThis way of describing an element is sufficient for this stage of your studies, but more details will be required as you progress through this course.Forexample,some elements contain isotopes.You will learn about isotopes inTopic 2. Neon contains three stable isotopes, 2°Ne, 21Ne,and 22Ne. All isotopes of the same elementhave the samenumberofprotons and electrons, but different numbers ofneutrons."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf017_0106", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "WHATISANATOM?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [4, 4], "pdf_pages": [17, 17], "char_count": 456, "text": "WHATISANATOM?\n\nYou alreadyknowwhat an atom is,becausewehaveused the term tohelpyou understandwhat an element is.Atoms are far toosmall tobeseenwith thehuman eye.You can see a tinygrain of sand and this contains many billions of atoms of silicon and oxygen.An atom canbe described as the smallestpart of an is fine when you work through Topic 1. However, in Topic 2 youwill needtorecallthatatomscontainevensmallerparticles (protons, neutrons and electrons)."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf017_0107", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "WHATISAMOLECULE?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [4, 4], "pdf_pages": [17, 17], "char_count": 744, "text": "WHATISAMOLECULE?\n\nThisis easiernow thatweknow the meanings of element and atom.\n\nYoucoulddescribeamoleculeasaparticlemadeoftwoormore atomsbonded together.\n\nIfamoleculecontainsatomsofthesameelement,thenthe resultis a molecule ofanelement.Forexample,a molecule that contains two atoms of hydrogen joined together can be represented by the formula H2. This formula contains only one symbol (H), so it is the formula of an element.\n\nIf amolecule contains atoms oftwo ormoredifferentelements, then the result is a molecule of a compound.Forexample,a molecule that contains two atoms of hydrogen and one atom of oxygenjoinedtogethercanberepresentedbytheformulaH2O. Thisformula contains two different symbols,so it is the formula ofa compound (water)."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf017_0108", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "WHATISACOMPOUND?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [4, 4], "pdf_pages": [17, 17], "char_count": 310, "text": "WHATISACOMPOUND?\n\nNow we can describe a compound. This is a substance containing atoms of different elements combined together.\n\nNote that some compounds containlargenumbers of atoms bonded together, but other compounds contain molecules with only will explore these differences in later sections of this book."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf017_0109", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "WHATISANION?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [4, 4], "pdf_pages": [17, 17], "char_count": 429, "text": "WHATISANION?\n\nOneway to describe anion is as a species consisting ofone or more\n\nNote that this is not a description of how an ion is formed. Topic 3A.1 will cover the formation of ions.\n\nAnionwith apositivecharge iscalled a cation.Anionwith a negative charge is called an anion.\n\nTableAshows diagramstoillustratethetermsreferred to.Each atomisshown asacirclecontainingthesymbolofanelement. The lines showbondsbetween atoms.\n\n1.1"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf018_0110", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "WHATISANION?", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [5, 5], "pdf_pages": [18, 18], "char_count": 925, "text": "table A Illustrations of terms used in thistopic.\n\n| TERM     | DIAGRAM   | NAME             | SYMBOLORFORMULA   | NOTE                                                       |\n|----------|-----------|------------------|-------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| element  |           | copper           | Cu                | This is an element. All the atoms are the same.            |\n| atom     | He        | helium           | He                | Thisis anatomofanelement                                   |\n| molecule |           | bromine          | Br2               | This is a molecule of anelement.The atoms are the same.    |\n| compound |           | hydrogen bromide | HBr               | This is a molecule of a compound. The atoms are different. |\n| ion      |           | carbonate        | CO2-              | This is an ion. There are two negative charges shown.      |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf018_0111", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "WHATISANION?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [5, 5], "pdf_pages": [18, 18], "char_count": 49, "text": "table A Illustrations of terms used in thistopic."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf018_0112", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "OTHERTERMS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [5, 5], "pdf_pages": [18, 18], "char_count": 329, "text": "OTHERTERMS\n\nElements that are made up of single atoms are described as monatomic. One example is helium, the gas used in weather balloons. The symbol for helium is He.\n\nElementsandcompoundsmadeupoftwo atomsjoined together are described as diatomic. The two main diatomic gases in the atmosphere are nitrogen (N2) and oxygen (O2)."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf018_0113", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [5, 5], "pdf_pages": [18, 18], "char_count": 588, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1.Classifyeachof these symbols andformulaeas atoms, moleculesorions.\n\nNe CO2\n\nH+\n\nS8\n\nA13+\n\n• 2.Whichof theseformulaerepresent elements,compounds, orneitherelementsnorcompounds?Explainyour answer. Br2 H2O2 NO3 O3 CaO\n\nElements andcompoundswithmoleculesmadeupofseveral atomsjoined together aredescribed aspolyatomic.Examples of polyatomic molecules are phosphorus (P4) and methane (CH4).\n\nThe same terms can be used forions. Chloride (Cl-) is an example of a monatomic ion. Hydroxide (HO- or OH-) is a diatomic ion. A sulfate ion (SO) is polyatomic as it contains five atoms."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf018_0114", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [5, 5], "pdf_pages": [18, 18], "char_count": 222, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nItisoftenhelpful torefertoasubstancebyitssymbolorformulaas well asitsnamesothatyourmeaningisclear.Forexample,theword hydrogen could refer to a hydrogen atom (H),a hydrogen molecule (H2) ora hydrogen ion (H+)."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c1_pdf018_0115", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 1, "chapter_title": "1A Atoms, elements and molecules", "section": "DID YOU KNOW?", "content_type": "did_you_know", "book_pages": [5, 5], "pdf_pages": [18, 18], "char_count": 383, "text": "DID YOU KNOW?\n\nThe names and symbolsofmany elementscomefromtheGreek andLatinlanguages.Forexample,hydrogencomesfromthe Greekwordsforwaterandproducer.Thisisbecausewhen hydrogen gas is burned,it forms(or'produces') water. Another example is copper(Cu)-the symbol Cu comes from the Latin word cuprum,which means'metal fromCyprusCyprus is theislandwheretheRomansobtainedmuchoftheircopper."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf019_0116", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [6, 6], "pdf_pages": [19, 19], "char_count": 106, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Writebalanced full and ionicequations,includingstate symbols,forchemical reactions."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf019_0117", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "WRITINGEQUATIONS:WHATTOREMEMBER", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [6, 6], "pdf_pages": [19, 19], "char_count": 112, "text": "WRITINGEQUATIONS:WHATTOREMEMBER\n\nThistopicis a usefulsummary ofwhatyou areexpected todo whenyou write equations."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf019_0118", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "WRITINGFORMULAEFORNAMES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [6, 6], "pdf_pages": [19, 19], "char_count": 614, "text": "WRITINGFORMULAEFORNAMES\n\nYou maybegiven theformulae of unfamiliar compounds in a question. You need to work out the formulae of many familiar compoundsfromtheirnames,and torememberotherformulae. You alsoneed to showelements with the correctformulae.It is not possible to provide a complete list, but here are some examples.\n\nYouneedtorememberthat:\n\n• oxygen is O2 and not O\n\n• hydrogen is H and not H\n\n• nitrogen is N and not N\n\n• water isHO\n\n• sodium hydroxide is NaOH\n\n• nitric acid is HNO3.\n\nYoushouldbe abletoworkout that:\n\n• ·iron(Il) sulfate is FeSO4\n\n• iron(III) oxide is Fe2O3\n\n• calcium carbonate is CaCO3"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf019_0119", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "EXAM HINT", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [6, 6], "pdf_pages": [19, 19], "char_count": 175, "text": "EXAM HINT\n\nRemembertouseyourPeriodicTableintheexam.lfyouknowthe formulaofmagnesium sulfatethenyoucan assume strontium sulfate hasthesameformulabecauseMgandSrareinthesamegroup."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf019_0120", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "WRITINGANEQUATIONFROMADESCRIPTION", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [6, 6], "pdf_pages": [19, 19], "char_count": 1597, "text": "WRITINGANEQUATIONFROMADESCRIPTION\n\nYouwillneedtoconvertwordsintoformulaeanddecidewhich ones arereactants andwhichones areproducts.\n\nConsiderthis description:when carbon dioxidereacts with calcium hydroxide, calcium carbonate and water are formed. The wording of the description makes it clear that carbon dioxide and calcium are the products.\n\nNow you have to write the formulae in the correct places:\n\nCO₂ + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O\n\nThenextstepisbalancingtheequation.You need toaddupthe numbersof all theatomstomakesure that,foreachelement, thetotals arethesameonboth theleftand therightsideofthe equation.In this example,thereis one carbon,onecalcium,two\n\n1.3\n\nhydrogen and four oxygen atoms on each side, so the equation is alreadybalanced.\n\nHere is an example where the first equation you write is not balanced.\n\nThe description is:\n\nhydrogen peroxide decomposes to water and oxygen.\n\nThe formulae are:\n\nH₂O₂ → H₂O + O₂\n\nThis is already balanced for hydrogen, but not for oxygen. With careful practice, which sometimes involves guessing until you get it right, you should be able to write the coefficients needed to balance the equation. In this case, the balanced equation is:\n\n2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂\n\nMost equations arebalanced usingwhole-number coefficients,but usingfractions or decimalsis usually acceptable.Thisisespecially the case in organic chemistry.\n\nConsider thisunbalanced equationfor the complete combustion of butane:\n\nC₄H₁₀ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O\n\nThe balanced equation can be either:\n\n2C₄H₁₀ + 13O₂ → 8CO₂ + 10H₂O\n\nor\n\nC₄H₁₀ + 6½O₂ → 4CO₂ + 5H₂O\n\nUsing 6.5 instead of 6 is also acceptable."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf019_0121", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "USINGSTATESYMBOLS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [6, 7], "pdf_pages": [19, 20], "char_count": 1018, "text": "USINGSTATESYMBOLS\n\nMany chemical equations include state symbols. The symbols are:\n\n• (s)=solid\n\n• (l)=liquid\n\n• (g) = gas\n\n• (aq) = aqueous (dissolved in water).\n\nIt is important to distinguish between (l) and(aq).A common error is towrite H2O(aq) instead of HzO(l).\n\nAlthoughit isgoodpracticetoincludestatesymbolsin all equations,insomecasestheyareessential,whileinothercases theyare oftenomitted.Forexample,whenwritingequations to representionisation energiesinTopic2A.4,it isimportant to include (g) after each atom and ion. However, in Topics 4 and 5 you will write equations for organic reactions, where state symbols are often not included.\n\nHere is another description:\n\nwhenaqueoussolutionsofsilvernitrateandcalcium chloride are mixed,a white precipitate of silver chloride forms.As this precipitate settles,a solution of calcium nitrate becomes visible.\n\nAfter writing the correct formulae, balancing the equation and including state symbols, the equation is:\n\n2AgNO(aq) + CaClz(aq) → 2AgCI(s) + Ca(NOg)2(aq)"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf020_0122", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "ARROWSINEQUATIONS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [7, 7], "pdf_pages": [20, 20], "char_count": 756, "text": "ARROWSINEQUATIONS\n\nMost equations are shown with a conventional (left to right) arrow →. However, some important reactions are reversible. This means that the reaction can go both in the forward and backward (reverse) directions.The symbol=is usedinequationsforthesereactions.You canfindguidancelaterin this book(Topic9B) aboutwhen thisreversible arrowshould beused.\n\nSometimes a conventional arrow is madelonger to allow information about the reaction tobe shown above the arrow(and sometimesbelowit). Thisinformation might be about reaction conditions,such astemperature,pressure and theuse ofa catalyst.In organicchemistry,wherereactionschemes are important, a label indicating,for example, Step 1, may be placed on the arrow in a sequence of reactions."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf020_0123", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "SIMPLIFYINGFULLEQUATIONS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [7, 7], "pdf_pages": [20, 20], "char_count": 373, "text": "SIMPLIFYINGFULLEQUATIONS\n\nIonic equations show any atoms and molecules involved, but only theions that react together, and not thespectatorions.\n\nThisistheeasiestmethod tofollowforsimplifyingequations:\n\n• 1Startwith thefull equationforthereaction.\n\n• 2Replace the formulae of ionic compounds by their separate ions.\n\n• 3Delete any ions that appearidentically on both sides."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf020_0124", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [7, 7], "pdf_pages": [20, 20], "char_count": 450, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1\n\nWhat is the simplest ionic equation for the neutralisation of sodium hydroxide solution by dilute nitric acid? The full equation is:\n\nNaOH(aq) +HNO3(aq)→NaNO3(aq) +H2O(I)\n\nYou should now consider which of these species are ionic and replace them with ions. In this example,the first threecompoundsareionic:\n\nNa(aq)+OH-(aq)+H+(aq)+NO(aq)→Na*(aq)+NO3(aq)+HO(1)\n\nAfterdeletingtheidentical ions,the equationbecomes:\n\nH+(aq)+OH-(aq)→H2O(l)"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf020_0125", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE2", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [7, 7], "pdf_pages": [20, 20], "char_count": 723, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE2\n\nWhat is the simplest ionic equationforthereaction that occurswhen solutions of lead(ll) nitrate and sodium sulfate react together to form a precipitate of lead(ll) sulfate and a solution of sodium nitrate?\n\nThe full equation is:\n\nPb(NO3)(aq)+Na2SO(aq)→PbSO4(s)+2NaNO3(aq)\n\nReplacing the appropriate species by ions gives:\n\nPb²+(aq)+2NO(aq)+2Na*(aq)+SO-(aq)→PbSO4(s)+2Na+(aq)+2NO3（aq)\n\nAfter deleting theidentical ions, the equation becomes:\n\nPb²+(aq)+SO²-(aq)→PbSO4(s)\n\nThese remainingions are not deleted because they arenot shown identically.Before thereaction they were free-moving ions in two separate solutions(Pb2+ and SO2-). After the reaction they are joined together in a solid precipitate (PbsO4)."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf021_0126", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [8, 8], "pdf_pages": [21, 21], "char_count": 206, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nConsidercarefullywhatthe correctstatesymbolfora speciesshouldbe.Itmaybe differentindifferentreactions. Forexample,waterisnever HO(aq),butitmaybeHO(s), HO(1)orHO（g),dependingon the temperature."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf021_0127", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [8, 8], "pdf_pages": [21, 21], "char_count": 347, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3\n\nThe full equation is:\n\nCO2(g)+Ca(OH)2(aq)→CaCO(s)+H2O(1)\n\nReplacing the appropriate species by ionsgives:\n\nCO2(g)+Ca²+（aq)+2OH-(aq)→CaCO(s)+H2O(1)\n\nNote that carbon dioxide and water are molecules,so their formulae are not changed.In this example. no ions are shown identically onboth sides, so this is the simplest ionic equation."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf021_0128", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "IONICHALF-EQUATIONS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [8, 8], "pdf_pages": [21, 21], "char_count": 345, "text": "IONICHALF-EQUATIONS\n\nWewriteionichalf-equationsforreactionsinvolving oxidation andreduction,and they usuallyshow what happens toonly onereactant.A simple example is thereaction that occurs at the negative electrode during the electrolysis of aqueous sulfuric acid:\n\n2H⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → H₂(g)\n\nYouwill learnmuchmore about ionichalf-equationsinTopic8."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf021_0129", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [8, 8], "pdf_pages": [21, 21], "char_count": 387, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1.Sodiumthiosulfate(Na2S2Os)solutionreactswithdilutehydrochloricacidtoforma precipitateof sulfur,gaseoussulfurdioxide anda solution of sodiumchloride.Write an equation,including state symbols,for this reaction.\n\n• 2.Solutionsofammoniumsulfateandsodiumhydroxidearewarmedtogethertoformsodium sulfatesolution,waterand ammoniagas.Writethesimplestionicequationfor this reaction."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf021_0130", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [8, 8], "pdf_pages": [21, 21], "char_count": 203, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\ncoefficientthetechnical termforthenumberwritteninfrontofspecieswhenbalancinganequation spectatorion an ion that is therebothbefore and after thereactionbutisnotinvolved in thereaction"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf022_0131", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [9, 9], "pdf_pages": [22, 22], "char_count": 133, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Relateionicandfullequations,withstatesymbols,toobservationsfromsimpletest-tubereactions,forreactions of acids."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf022_0132", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "INTRODUCTION", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [9, 9], "pdf_pages": [22, 22], "char_count": 264, "text": "INTRODUCTION\n\nAcids are common reagents in chemistry. In this topic,we summarise some of their typical reactions,using hydrochloric, nitric, sulfuric and phosphoric acids.\n\nIneach of these reactions,a salt is formed.The reactions can be usedtopreparesamplesofsalts"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf022_0133", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "ACIDSWITHMETALS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [9, 9], "pdf_pages": [22, 22], "char_count": 677, "text": "ACIDSWITHMETALS\n\nAgeneralequationforthesereactionsis:\n\nmetal + acid → salt + hydrogen\n\nBubbles of hydrogen gas form, and if the salt formed is soluble, then a solution forms.\n\nThe metal must be sufficiently reactive to react in this way. For example,magnesiumreactsbutcopper doesnot.\n\nTypical equations for magnesium and hydrochloric acid are:\n\nMg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ Mg(s) + 2H⁺(aq) → Mg²⁺(aq) + H₂(g)\n\nThese reactions may appear tobe examples of neutralisation reactionsbecausetheH+ions areremovedfrom thesolution when they react with the metal. However, as the H+ions gain electrons from the metal and are converted to Hz(g),it means that theH+ions arereduced,notneutralised"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf022_0134", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "ACIDSWITHMETALOXIDESANDINSOLUBLE METALHYDROXIDES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [9, 9], "pdf_pages": [22, 22], "char_count": 767, "text": "ACIDSWITHMETALOXIDESANDINSOLUBLE METALHYDROXIDES\n\nAgeneral equationfor thesereactions is:\n\nmetaloxide+acid→salt+water\n\nmetalhydroxide+acid→salt+water\n\nThereactivityof themetal doesnot matterin thesereactions because in the reactant it is present as metal ions,not metal atoms. The only observation is likely to be the formation of a solution.\n\nTypical equations for copper(Il) oxide and zinc hydroxide reacting with sulfuric acid are:\n\nCuO + H2SO4 → CuSO4 + H2O\n\nCuO(s) + 2H+(aq)→ Cu²+(aq) + H2O(l)\n\nZn(OH)2 + H2SO4 → ZnSO4 + 2H2O\n\nZn(OH)(s) + 2H+(aq)→Zn²+(aq) + 2H2O(l)\n\nThesereactions canbeclassified asneutralisationreactions because the H+ions react with O²-or OH-ions.They are not redoxreactionsbecause thereisno changein theoxidation numberof any ofthe species."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf022_0135", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "ACIDSWITHALKALIS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [9, 9], "pdf_pages": [22, 22], "char_count": 805, "text": "ACIDSWITHALKALIS\n\nMetalhydroxidesthatdissolveinwater arecalledalkalis.A general equation for these reactions is:\n\nalkali + acid → salt + water\n\nThere are no visible changes during these reactions, although if a thermometer is used, a temperature rise can be noted.\n\nTypical equations for sodium hydroxide reacting with phosphoric acid are:\n\nNaOH + HPO4→ NaHPO4 + HO\n\n2NaOH + HPO4→ NaHPO4 + 2HO\n\n3NaOH+HPO4→NaPO4+3HO\n\nThere are three replaceable hydrogens in phosphoric acid. The saltformed depends on therelative amounts of acid and alkali used.Theionicequationfor all thesereactionsis:\n\nH+(aq)+OH-(aq)→H2O(l)\n\nThesereactions canbe classified as neutralisationreactions because theH+ionsreact with OH-ions.They arenot redox reactions because thereis nochange in the oxidation number of any of the species."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf022_0136", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "ACIDSWITHCARBONATES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [9, 9], "pdf_pages": [22, 22], "char_count": 549, "text": "ACIDSWITHCARBONATES\n\nA general equation for these reactions is:\n\nmetalcarbonate+acid→salt+water+carbon dioxide\n\nBubbles of carbon dioxidegas form.If the salt formed is soluble, then a solution forms.\n\nTypical equations for lithium carbonate reacting with hydrochloric acid are:\n\nLi₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2LiCl + H₂O + CO₂ CO₃²⁻(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) → H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)\n\nThesereactionscanbeclassified asneutralisationreactions because theH+ionsreactwithCO-ions.They arenotredox reactions because there is no change in the oxidation number of anyof thespecies.\n\n1.12(i)"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf023_0137", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [10, 10], "pdf_pages": [23, 23], "char_count": 268, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nPractisewritingfull andionic equationsfordifferentreactions ofacids.\n\nfigAManyoldbuildings aremadefromcarbonatessuch aslimestone.Centuries ofreactionbetweenlimestone and acids in the atmosphere have caused damage to the walls of Kolossi Castle in Cyprus."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf023_0138", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "ACIDSWITHHYDROGENCARBONATES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [10, 10], "pdf_pages": [23, 23], "char_count": 724, "text": "ACIDSWITHHYDROGENCARBONATES\n\nHydrogencarbonates are compounds containing the hydrogencarbonate ion(HCOs), and they react (NaHCO), commonly known as bicarbonate of soda or baking soda.Baking soda is used in cooking at homeand in thefood industry.The'lightness'ofbakedfood such ascakes isdue to theformation of bubbles of carbon dioxidein the cake mixture,which cause the cake to rise.\n\nA word equation for the reaction between baking soda and the acid in lemon juice is:\n\nsodiumhydrogencarbonate+citric acid→sodium citrate+water+carbondioxide\n\nA suitable test for the presence of carbonate or hydrogencarbonateions in a solid or solution is to add an aqueous acid and to test the gas produced with limewater (see Topic 8B.4)."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf023_0139", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [10, 10], "pdf_pages": [23, 23], "char_count": 266, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1.Writefullequationsforthereactionsbetween:\n\n• (a)zinc and sulfuric acid\n\n• (b)aluminium oxide and hydrochloric acid.\n\n• 2.Writethe simplestionicequationsforthereactionsbetween:\n\n• (a)zincand hydrochloricacid\n\n• (b) magnesium carbonate and nitric acid."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf024_0140", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [11, 11], "pdf_pages": [24, 24], "char_count": 148, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Relate ionic and full equations,with state symbols,to observations from simple test-tube reactions,for displacementreactions."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf024_0141", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "WHATISADISPLACEMENTREACTION?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [11, 11], "pdf_pages": [24, 24], "char_count": 397, "text": "WHATISADISPLACEMENTREACTION?\n\nAsyoulearnaboutmorechemicalreactions,youwillkmowthat theyareoften classifiedintodifferent typesofreaction.You will recognise reaction types such as addition, neutralisation, combustion,oxidation andseveral others.\n\nIn this topic,we look at a reaction type called displacement. In simple terms, it is a reaction in which one element replaces anotherelementinacompound."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf024_0142", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "DISPLACEMENTREACTIONSINVOLVINGMETALS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [11, 11], "pdf_pages": [24, 24], "char_count": 877, "text": "DISPLACEMENTREACTIONSINVOLVINGMETALS\n\nHere are the equations for two displacement reactions of metals:\n\nReaction 1Mg(s) + CuSO4(aq) → Cu(s) + MgSO4(aq)\n\nReaction 22Al(s)+Fe2O3(s)→2Fe(s)+ Al2O3(s)\n\nWhatdo thesereactionshave in common?\n\n• Both involve one metal reacting with the compound of a different metal.\n\n• Both produce a metal and a different metal compound.\n\n• Both are redoxreactions.\n\n• Youcanseethatthemetalelementonthereactantssidehas taken the place of the metal in the metal compound on the reactantsside.\n\nWhatarethedifferencesbetweenthereactions?\n\n• Reaction1 takesplace in aqueous solution,but Reaction 2 involves only solids.\n\n• Reaction 1 occurs without the need for energy to be supplied, but Reaction 2 requires a very high temperature to start it.\n\n• Reaction1is likely tobe done in the laboratory,but Reaction 2 is done for a specific purpose in industry."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf024_0143", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "METALDISPLACEMENTREACTIONSINAQUEOUSSOLUTION", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [11, 11], "pdf_pages": [24, 24], "char_count": 765, "text": "METALDISPLACEMENTREACTIONSINAQUEOUSSOLUTION\n\nNowyoucanseethatthisis aredoxreaction.Electrons are transferred from magnesium atoms to copper(Il) ions, so magnesium atoms are oxidised (loss of electrons) and copper(II) ions are reduced (gain of electrons).\n\nThis reaction is just one example of many similar reactions in which amorereactivemetaldisplacesalessreactivemetalfromoneof its salts.You may come across this and other similar reactions as examples used in the measurement of temperature changes.\n\n• fig AThe photo shows what happens when a copper wire is placed insilvernitratesolutionforsome time.You cansee theresultsof the displacement reaction, the'growth' on the wire is silver metal and the blue solution contains the copper(ll) nitrate that is formed."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf024_0144", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "METALDISPLACEMENTREACTIONSINTHESOLIDSTATE", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [11, 11], "pdf_pages": [24, 24], "char_count": 1028, "text": "METALDISPLACEMENTREACTIONSINTHESOLIDSTATE\n\nTake a closer look at Reaction 1, shown above.When magnesium metal is added to copper(Il) sulfate solution,the blue colour of the solution becomes paler If an excess of magnesium is added, the solution becomes colourless, as magnesium sulfate forms. The magnesium changes in appearance from silvery to brown as copperforms on it.\n\nReaction 2 is used in the railway industry to join rails together You welding,but the metal rails aregood conductors of heat and it is melt and join together\n\nThe equation can be rewritten as an ionic equation:\n\nMg(s) + Cu²+(aq) + SO2-(aq)→ Cu(s) +Mg²+(aq)+SO2-(aq)\n\nMg(s) + Cu²+(aq)→ Cu(s) + Mg²+(aq)\n\nFor this reason,the thermite method is used.A mixture of aluminium and iron(IIl) oxide ispositioned just above theplace where the two rails are to be joined. A magnesium fuse is lit, and Reaction 2occurs.It issoexothermic that theironisformed as a molten metal, which flows into thegapbetween the two rails. The moltenironcools,joiningtherailstogether"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf025_0145", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [12, 12], "pdf_pages": [25, 25], "char_count": 802, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nWhendescribingdisplacement reactions,be carefultoreferto the correct species.For example,inthereaction betweenmagnesiumand copper(ll)sulfate,magnesium atoms andcopper(ll) ionsare involved.\n\nAs in Reaction 1, the equation can be rewritten ionically and simplified:\n\n2Al(l) + 2Fe³⁺(l) + 3O²⁻(l) → 2Fe(l) + 2Al³⁺(l) + 3O²⁻(l)\n\nthen:\n\n2Al(l) + 2Fe³⁺(l) → 2Fe(l) + 2Al³⁺(l)\n\nAswithReaction 1,youcansee that this is s aredoxreaction.Electrons are transferredfrom aluminium atoms to iron(IIl) ions, so aluminium atoms are oxidised (loss of electrons) and iron(III) ions arereduced(gainofelectrons).\n\n• figBThe thermite reaction.The flame comes from thehighly exothermicreaction forming molten iron.The molten iron will be used to fillthe gapbetween two rails and form a strong join between them."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf025_0146", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "DISPLACEMENTREACTIONSINVOLVINGHALOGENS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [12, 12], "pdf_pages": [25, 25], "char_count": 614, "text": "DISPLACEMENTREACTIONSINVOLVINGHALOGENS\n\nIn Topic8C.2,we willlook at how more reactive halogenscan displace less reactive halogensfrom their compounds. For example, chlorine will displace bromine from a potassium bromide solution. The full, ionic and simplified ionic equations for thisreaction are:\n\nCl2(aq) + 2KBr(aq) → Br2(aq) + 2KCl(aq)\n\nCl2(aq)+2K+(aq)+2Br(aq)→Br2(aq)+2K+(aq)+2Cl-(aq)\n\n()+ )()+())\n\nAs with themetal displacementreactions,this is aredoxreaction.Electrons are transferredfrom bromide ions to chlorine,so bromide ions are oxidised (loss of electrons) and chlorine is reduced (gain of electrons)."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf025_0147", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [12, 12], "pdf_pages": [25, 25], "char_count": 437, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1.lronmetalreactswithsilvernitrateinadisplacementreactiontoformsilverandiron(ll)nitrate.Writea fullequation,anionicequationandasimplifiedionicequationforthisreaction.Includestatesymbols in all your equations.\n\n• 2.Amixture ofzincmetalandcopper(ll)oxideisignited,causinganexothermicreactionto occur.Writea fullequation,anionicequation and a simplifiedionicequationforthisreaction.Donotincludestate symbols in your equations."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf025_0148", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [12, 12], "pdf_pages": [25, 25], "char_count": 114, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\ndisplacementreactionareactioninwhichoneelementreplacesanother,lessreactive,elementin acompound"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf026_0149", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [13, 13], "pdf_pages": [26, 26], "char_count": 287, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Relate ionic and full equations,with state symbols,to observations from simple test-tube reactions,for precipitation reactions.\n\nIn this topic,wefocus on twoaspects ofprecipitationreactions:\n\n• their use inchemical tests\n\n• theiruseinworkingout chemical equations."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf026_0150", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "CARBONDIOXIDE", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [13, 13], "pdf_pages": [26, 26], "char_count": 507, "text": "CARBONDIOXIDE\n\nThis may be your earliest memory of a precipitation reaction.When carbon dioxide gas is bubbled through calcium hydroxide solution(often called limewater),a white precipitate of calcium carbonate forms.The relevant equation is:\n\nCa(OH)(aq) + CO2(g) → CaCO(s) +H2O(l)\n\nThe formation of the white precipitate was probably described as the limewater going milky or cloudy.\n\nfig ALimewater isa colourlesssolution.Asmore carbon dioxideisbubbled throughit,the amount of white precipitate increases."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf026_0151", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "SULFATES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [13, 13], "pdf_pages": [26, 26], "char_count": 420, "text": "SULFATES\n\nThe presence of sulfate ions in solution can be shown by the addition of barium ions(usually from solutions of barium chloride or barium nitrate). The white precipitate that forms is barium sulfate.\n\nForexample,whenbarium chloridesolutionis addedtosodiumsulfatesolution,therelevant equationsare:\n\nNa2SO4(aq) +BaClz(aq)→BaSO4(s) + 2NaCl(aq)\n\nSO2-(aq) +Ba2+(aq)→BaSO(s)\n\nThistestiscoveredinmoredetailinTopic8B.4."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf027_0152", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "HALIDES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [14, 14], "pdf_pages": [27, 27], "char_count": 398, "text": "HALIDES\n\nThe presence of halideions in solution can be shown by the addition of silverions(from silver nitrate solution). The precipitates that form are silver halides.\n\nFor example, when silver nitrate solution is added to sodium chloride solution, the relevant equations are:\n\nNaCl(aq) + AgNOs(aq)→AgCl(s) + NaNOs(aq)\n\nCl-(aq) + Ag+(aq) → AgCl(s)\n\nThis test is covered in more detail inTopic8C.4."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf027_0153", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "WORKINGOUTEQUATIONS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [14, 14], "pdf_pages": [27, 27], "char_count": 859, "text": "WORKINGOUTEQUATIONS\n\nA good example of using a precipitation reaction to work out an equation is the reaction between aqueous solutions of lead nitrate and potassium iodide.Both reactants are colourless solutions. When they are mixed, a yellow precipitate of lead iodide forms.\n\nThe word equationforthis reaction is:\n\nlead nitrate+potassium iodide→lead iodide+potassium nitrate\n\nHereis anoutline of theexperiment.\n\n• ·Place the samevolume of a potassiumiodide solution in a series oftest tubes.\n\n• ·Adddifferentvolumesofaleadnitratesolutiontothetubes.\n\n• ·Place each tube in a centrifuge and spin the tubes for the same length of time.\n\n• ·Measure the depth of precipitate in each tube.\n\nTable A shows the results of one experiment.\n\nThe concentration of both solutions is 1.0 mol dm-3.\n\nThe depth of each precipitate indicates the mass of precipitate formed."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf027_0154", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "WORKINGOUTEQUATIONS", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [14, 14], "pdf_pages": [27, 27], "char_count": 424, "text": "| TUBE                                   |   1 |   2 |   3 |   4 |   5 |   6 |   7 |\n|----------------------------------------|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|\n| volumeofpotassiumiodide solution / cm3 | 5.0 | 5.0 | 5.0 | 5.0 | 5.0 | 5.0 | 5.0 |\n| volumeof leadnitrate solution/ cm3     | 0.5 | 1.0 | 1.5 | 2.0 | 2.5 | 3.0 | 3.5 |\n| depth of precipitate/ cm               | 2.5 |   3 |   4 |   5 |   6 |   6 |   6 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf027_0155", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "WORKINGOUTEQUATIONS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [14, 14], "pdf_pages": [27, 27], "char_count": 239, "text": "table AResults of thereactionbetween aqueous solutions of lead nitrate andpotassium iodidein one experiment.\n\nThe diagram shows the tubes at the end of the experiment.\n\nfigBAsmoreleadnitratesolution isadded,morelead iodideprecipitateforms."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf028_0156", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "1B.4PRECIPITATIONREACTIONS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [15, 15], "pdf_pages": [28, 28], "char_count": 519, "text": "1B.4PRECIPITATIONREACTIONS\n\nYoucan see that there is noincrease in the amount ofprecipitatefrom tube5totube6.Thisshows that the reaction is incomplete in tubes 1,2,3 and 4,but is complete in tube 5.The amounts,in moles,ofreactantsusedintube5 are calculated asfollows:\n\nn(potassium iodide) = 0.005× 1.0 = 0.005mol\n\nn(lead nitrate)= 0.0025×1.0 =0.0025mol\n\nThis shows that lead nitratereactswithpotassiumiodidein theratio1: 2.\n\nThe equations for the reaction are:\n\n(b)ONXZ + (s) (be)I7 + ()(N)\n\nPb2+(aq) + 2I-(aq) →PbI2(s)"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf028_0157", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [15, 15], "pdf_pages": [28, 28], "char_count": 76, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nPractisecalculatingtheamountsofreactantsandproductsintubes1-4."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf028_0158", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [15, 15], "pdf_pages": [28, 28], "char_count": 211, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1. Write thesimplest ionic equation,includingstate symbols,for:\n\n• (a)thetestforasulfate\n\n• (b)the test fora chloride.\n\n• Calculate the amounts,inmoles,of eachreactant and product in tube7in fig B."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c2_pdf028_0159", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 2, "chapter_title": "1B Equations and reaction types", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [15, 15], "pdf_pages": [28, 28], "char_count": 110, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nprecipitationreactionreaction inwhich aninsolublesolid isformedwhen two solutions aremixed"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf029_0160", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [16, 16], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 264, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Understand the terms: relative atomic mass, based on the 12C scale; relative molecular mass; relative formula mass; molar mass, as the mass per mole of a substance in g mol-1.\n\n• Performcalculationsusing theAvogadroconstantL(6.02x1023mol-1)."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf029_0161", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "RELATIVEATOMICMASS(A.)", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [16, 16], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 1156, "text": "RELATIVEATOMICMASS(A.)\n\nAschemistsdiscoveredmoreandmoreelementsinthenineteenth century,theybegantorealise thatthemasses of theelements were abletousenumberstocompare themassesofatomsof differentelements.For thisreason,theybegantouse theterm 'relative atomic mass'.\n\nThechemistssoonrealised thattheelementwhoseatomshad thesmallest masswashydrogen,so therelative atomicmass of hydrogenwas fixed as 1.Atoms of silicon had double themass ofnitrogen atoms,and nitrogen atomswere14timesheavier than hydrogen atoms. This meant that the relative atomic mass of nitrogenwas14,and thatof siliconwas28.Atfirst,mostlywhole numberswere used,but eventually it waspossible tofind themass of an atom to several decimal places.ThePeriodic Table in the DataBookletuses1decimalplaceforlighterelements andwhole numbers for heavier ones.\n\nAfterthediscoveryofisotopes,the12Cisotopeofcarbonwas used in thedefinition ofrelative atomicmass.\n\nAsuitabledefinition ofrelativeatomicmassis:\n\nthe weighted mean (average) mass of an atom compared to of the mass of an atom of 12C It isoftenusefultorememberthis expression:\n\nAr meanmassofanatomofanelement\n\n12 of the mass of an atom of 12C"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf029_0162", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "RELATIVEMOLECULARMASS(M)", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [16, 16], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 277, "text": "RELATIVEMOLECULARMASS(M)\n\nRelativeatomicmassesareusedforatoms ofelements.Relative molecular masses are usedfor molecules ofbothelements and compounds. They are easily calculated by adding relative atomic masses.\n\nTableAshowsvaluesforsomecommonelementstakenfrom the DataBooklet."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf029_0163", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "RELATIVEMOLECULARMASS(M)", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [16, 16], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 258, "text": "| ELEMENT   |   RELATIVEATOMICMASS |\n|-----------|----------------------|\n| hydrogen  |                  1.0 |\n| carbon    |                 12.0 |\n| oxygen    |                 16.0 |\n| sulfur    |                 32.1 |\n| copper    |                 63.5 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf029_0164", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "RELATIVEMOLECULARMASS(M)", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [16, 16], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 77, "text": "Note that A and M,do not have units.\n\nHere are some examples of calculations."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf029_0165", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [16, 16], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 200, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1\n\nWhatis therelativemolecularmassof carbondioxide,CO? M=12.0+(2×16.0)=44.0\n\nWORKEDEXAMPLE2\n\nWhatis the relative molecularmassof sulfuric acid,HSO?\n\nM, = (2×1.0) + 32.1 + (4 ×16.0) = 98.1"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf029_0167", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "EXAM HINT", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [16, 16], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 90, "text": "EXAM HINT\n\nMakesureyouusetherelativeatomicmassesshownonthePeriodic Tablein theDataBooklet."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf029_0168", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "RELATIVEFORMULAMASS(M)", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [16, 16], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 425, "text": "RELATIVEFORMULAMASS(M)\n\nThis term has the same symbol as relative molecular mass, but the formula' part means that it includes both molecules and ions. Workedexample3belowisslightlymorecomplicatedbecause ofthewater ofcrystallisation,but thereis alsoanotherproblem Hydrated copper(II) sulfate is an ionic compound, so it is not a good idea to refer to its relative molecular mass. That is why it is calledrelativeformula mass."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf029_0169", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [16, 16], "pdf_pages": [29, 29], "char_count": 276, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3\n\nWhat is the relative formula mass of hydrated copper(ll) sulfate, CuSO4.5H2O?\n\nM, = 63.5 + 32.1 + (4 ×16.0) + 5(2 ×1.0) + 16.0 = 249.6\n\nTheterm'relativeformulamass'should alsobeusedfor compounds withgiant structures,such as sodium chloride and silicon dioxide."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf029_0170", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "MOLARMASS(M)", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [16, 17], "pdf_pages": [29, 30], "char_count": 495, "text": "MOLARMASS(M)\n\nAnotherwayaroundtheprobleminWorkedexample3is to use theterm molar mass,whichis the massper mole of any substance(molecular orionic). Its symbol is M(not M) and it has the units gmol- (grams per mole). Here we have a new term (the mole) which will be fully explained in Topic 1C.2. For now, you can think of one mole (1 mol) of a substance asbeing the same quantity as the relative formula mass of the substance,with the units of grams.\n\n1.4\n\nSo, this is the expression you can use:"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf030_0171", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "CALCULATIONSUSINGTHEAVOGADROCONSTANT", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [17, 17], "pdf_pages": [30, 30], "char_count": 379, "text": "CALCULATIONSUSINGTHEAVOGADROCONSTANT\n\namount in mol = mass of substance in g ÷ molar mass in g mol⁻¹, or n = m/M\n\nYouwill need touse thevalue ofLin the typesofcalculation shown here.\n\nTable B shows examples of working out the amounts in moles of some substances using this expression.\n\n• 1Calculate thenumber ofparticlesin agivenmass of a substance.Start by using the expression:"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf030_0172", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "CALCULATIONSUSINGTHEAVOGADROCONSTANT", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [17, 17], "pdf_pages": [30, 30], "char_count": 314, "text": "| SUBSTANCE                |    02 |   CH4 |   H20 |   NHNO3 |\n|--------------------------|-------|-------|-------|---------|\n| mass in g                |  5.26 |   4.0 |   100 |    14.7 |\n| molar mass, M in g mol-1 |  32.0 |  16.0 |  18.0 |    80.0 |\n| amount in mol            | 0.164 |  0.25 |  5.56 |   0.184 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf030_0173", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "THEAVOGADROCONSTANT", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [17, 17], "pdf_pages": [30, 30], "char_count": 891, "text": "THEAVOGADROCONSTANT\n\nAmedeoAvogadro(1776-1856)was anItalianchemistwhose name isused innamingtheAvogadroconstant.We are introducinghim herebecause the scaling-upfactorfrom atoms, molecules and ions to grams is named after him.\n\nfig ATheItalian chemist,Amedeo Avogadro\n\nThe value of the Avogadro constant is approximately 602000000000000000000000mol-1.Itiseasiertowritethis number using standard form: 6.02 x 1023 mol-1.\n\nYou do not need to know a definition of the Avogadro constant, and it is best to think of it as the number of particles (atoms, molecules or ions) in one mole of any substance. For example, there are:\n\n6.02 × 1023 helium atoms in 4.0g of He\n\n6.02 × 1023 carbon dioxide molecules in 44.0g of CO2\n\n6.02 × 1023 nitrate ions in 62.0 g of NO\n\namount in mol = mass of substance in g ÷ molar mass in g mol⁻¹, or n = m/M\n\nthen multiply the amount in mol by the Avogadro constant."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf030_0174", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE4", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [17, 17], "pdf_pages": [30, 30], "char_count": 519, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE4\n\nHow many H2O molecules are there in 1.25g of water?\n\nn = 1.25 ÷ 18.0 = 0.0694 mol\n\nnumber of molecules = 6.02 ×1023 ×0.0694 = 4.18 ×1022\n\n• 2Calculate the mass of a given number of particles of a substance: start by dividing the number of particles by the Avogadro constant, then multiply the result by the molar mass.\n\nWORKEDEXAMPLE5\n\nWhat is the mass of 100 million atoms of gold?\n\n100×106 n= = 1.66×10-16mol 6.02×1023\n\nm= 1.66×10-16×197.0= 3.27×10-14g\n\n(There are lots of atoms,but only a tiny mass.)"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf030_0176", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [17, 17], "pdf_pages": [30, 30], "char_count": 194, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1.Malachiteis animportant mineral with the formula CuCO(OH)2 Calculateitsrelativeformulamass.\n\n• 2. How many molecules of sugar(Ci2H22O11) are there in a teaspoon measure (4.20 g)?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf030_0177", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "DID YOU KNOW?", "content_type": "did_you_know", "book_pages": [17, 17], "pdf_pages": [30, 30], "char_count": 308, "text": "DID YOU KNOW?\n\nThe symbol Lis usedfor theAvogadro constant(usingA would be confusingbecauseof theuseofA,forrelative atomicmass). LcomesfromthesurnameofJohannJosefLoschmidt (1821-1895),anAustrianchemistwhowasa contemporaryof Avogadro.He mademany contributions toour understanding of the same areaof knowledge."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf030_0178", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [17, 17], "pdf_pages": [30, 30], "char_count": 171, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nmolarmassthemasspermoleofasubstance;ithasthesymbol M and the units gmol-1\n\nAvogadroconstant(L)6.02x1023mol-1,thenumberofparticlesin onemoleofasubstance"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf031_0179", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [18, 18], "pdf_pages": [31, 31], "char_count": 89, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Know that the mole (mol) is the unit for the amount ofa substance."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf031_0180", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WHATISAMOLE?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [18, 18], "pdf_pages": [31, 31], "char_count": 138, "text": "WHATISAMOLE?\n\nSofarwehavereferred to themole and have used itina simple form of calculation,but we have not properly explained what it is."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf031_0181", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "THEDEFINITIONOFAMOLE", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [18, 18], "pdf_pages": [31, 31], "char_count": 348, "text": "THEDEFINITIONOFAMOLE\n\nAmoleisthe amountofsubstance that containsthesame number ofparticles asthenumber ofcarbon atomsinexactly 12g of the1²C isotope.\n\nThis definition is noteasy to understand,but will be explained as you read on.\n\nfig A From left to right: tin (Sn).magnesium (Mg).iodine (I) and copper (Cu). Each sample contains 6.02 × 1023 atoms."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf031_0182", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "COUNTINGATOMS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [18, 18], "pdf_pages": [31, 31], "char_count": 1196, "text": "COUNTINGATOMS\n\nAs you know, atoms are very tiny particles that cannot be seen by the human eye. When we look at the sand on a beach, we are looking at billions and billions of atoms of, mostly, silicon and oxygen in the compound silicon dioxide (SiO2). Quoting the actual numbers of atomsinvolved in a reaction,whether in a test tube or on anindustrial scale,would involve extremely large numbers that would be very difficult to handle.\n\nYouare alreadyfamiliar withtheuse ofrelative atomicmasses to compare the relative masses of atoms.In a water molecule,the oxygen atom has a mass that is 16 times greater than the mass of a hydrogen atom.Youknow thisbecauseinthePeriodicTable the relative atomic masses are H = 1.0 and O =16.0. Note the word 'relative' These values donot tell us the actual mass in grams of an atom has a mass 16 times greater than that of a hydrogen atom.\n\nNow consider using these numbers(16.0 and 1.0) with the familiar unit g (grams). You can more easily visualise 16.0g of oxygen than a singleatomofoxygen.Doingthisiseffectivelyscalingupon a very large scale. The number of oxygen atoms in 16.0 g of oxygen is the same as the number of hydrogen atoms in 1.0g of hydrogen."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf031_0183", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WHATTOREMEMBERWHENDOINGCALCULATIONS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [18, 18], "pdf_pages": [31, 31], "char_count": 576, "text": "WHATTOREMEMBERWHENDOINGCALCULATIONS\n\nYou can use themole to count atoms,molecules,ions,electrons and otherspecies.So,it is important toinclude anexact description of the species being referred to. Consider the examplesofhydrogen,oxygenandwater.\n\nOne mole of water has a mass of 18.0g, but what is the mass of one mole of hydrogen or oxygen? It depends on whether you are referringtoatoms ormolecules,soyou need tomake thisclear.\n\nRememberthatthesymbolnisusedfortheamountofsubstance in mol.\n\nConsider the substancesintableA.Themasses are the same,but the amounts are different."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf031_0184", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WHATTOREMEMBERWHENDOINGCALCULATIONS", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [18, 18], "pdf_pages": [31, 31], "char_count": 379, "text": "|           | 1gOFOXYGEN ATOMS(O)   | 1gOFOXYGEN MOLECULES(02)   | 1gOFOZONE MOLECULES(03)   |\n|-----------|-----------------------|----------------------------|---------------------------|\n| n (amount | 1 + 16.0=             | 1 ÷ 32.0 =                 | 1 ÷ 48.0 =                |\n| in mol)   | 0.0625mol             | 0.0313mol                  | 0.0208mol                 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf031_0185", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "tableA", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [18, 18], "pdf_pages": [31, 31], "char_count": 604, "text": "tableA\n\nYou should alwaysclearlyidentifythe species thatyou are referring to if there is anypossibility that there couldbe more than onemeaning. This is not usually necessary for compounds.\n\nIt is good practice to refer to both the formula and the name. Examples include:\n\n• the amount, in moles, of O in 9.4g of oxygen atoms (0.59mol)\n\n• the amount, in moles,of O2 in 9.4g of oxygen molecules (0.29mol)\n\n• the amount, in moles,of Oin 9.4g of ozone molecules (0.20mol)\n\n• the amount, in moles, of CO2 in 9.4g of carbon dioxide (0.21 mol)\n\n• the amount, in moles, of SO2-in 9.4g of sulfate ions (0.098mol)"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf031_0186", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "THEEQUATIONFORCALCULATINGMOLES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [18, 18], "pdf_pages": [31, 31], "char_count": 275, "text": "THEEQUATIONFORCALCULATINGMOLES\n\nThe equation for calculatingmoles is:\n\namount of substance in moles = mass in grams ÷ molar mass, or n = m/M\n\nYou will use this expression in many calculations during your study of chemistry. It is often rearranged as:\n\nM = m ÷ n, or m = n × M"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf031_0187", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [18, 19], "pdf_pages": [31, 32], "char_count": 322, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1\n\nWhatisthe amountofsubstancein6.51gofsodiumchloride?\n\nn = m ÷ M = 6.51 ÷ 58.5 = 0.111 mol\n\nWORKEDEXAMPLE2\n\nWhatisthemassof0.263mol ofhydrogeniodide? m=n ×M=0.263×127.9=33.6g\n\nWORKEDEXAMPLE3\n\nAsampleof0.284mol ofa substancehasa massof17.8g.What isthemolarmassof thesubstance?\n\nm 17.8 = 62.7 gmol-1 M= n 0.284"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf032_0190", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [19, 19], "pdf_pages": [32, 32], "char_count": 145, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nWhenusingmoles,alwaysmakeclearwhatparticlesyouarereferringto:atoms,molecules,ionsor electrons.Itis alsoagoodideatostate theformula."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf032_0191", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [19, 19], "pdf_pages": [32, 32], "char_count": 340, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1.What isthe amountof substanceineachof thefollowing?\n\n• (a)8.00g of sulfur,S\n\n• (b）8.00g of sulfur dioxide,SO2\n\n• (b)8.00 g of sulfate ions, SO2-\n\n• How many particles are there of the specified substance?\n\n• (a)atomsin2.00gof sulfur,S\n\n• (b)molecules in 4.00 g of sulfur dioxide,SO2\n\n• (b) ions in 8.00g of sulfate ions, SO2"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf032_0192", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [19, 19], "pdf_pages": [32, 32], "char_count": 126, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nmoletheamountofsubstancethatcontainsthesamenumberofparticlesasthenumberofcarbonatoms in exactly 12g of 12c"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf033_0193", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [20, 20], "pdf_pages": [33, 33], "char_count": 218, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Usechemicalequationstocalculatereactingmassesandviceversa,usingtheconceptsofamountofsubstance andmolarmass.\n\n• Determine a formula orconfirm anequationbyexperiment,including evaluationof thedata."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf033_0194", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "INTRODUCTIONTOREACTINGMASSES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [20, 20], "pdf_pages": [33, 33], "char_count": 1135, "text": "INTRODUCTIONTOREACTINGMASSES\n\nYou can use the ideas from previous topics about amounts of substance and equations to do calculations involving the masses of reactants and products in equations.\n\nA balanced equationfor a reaction shows the same number of each species(atoms,molecules,ions or electrons)onbothsidesof theequation.Itis alsobalancedfor themasses ofeachspecies.This meansthatwe can makepredictions about themasses ofreactants,whichareneeded toform a specified mass or amount of a product, or the other way round.\n\nConsider this equation used in themanufacture of ammonia:\n\nN₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃\n\nThis shows thatonemolecule of nitrogen reactswith three molecules of hydrogen toform two molecules of ammonia.\n\nThis statement can be made about the amounts involved:\n\n1 mol of N reacts with 3mol of H to form 2mol of NH3.\n\nThisstatement canbemade aboutthemassesinvolved:\n\n28.0g of N2 reacts with 6.0g of H2 to form 34.0g of NH3\n\nThese amounts and masses can have many other values, as long as the ratio does not change.\n\nCALCULATINGREACTINGMASSESFROMEQUATIONS\n\nUsing a balanced equation, predictions can be made about reacting masses."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf033_0196", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [20, 20], "pdf_pages": [33, 33], "char_count": 559, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1\n\nThe equationfora reaction is: SO+HO→HSO4 Whatmassofsulfurtrioxideisneededtoform75.0gofsulfuricacid? Stepl:calculate themolarmassesofall substancesyou aretold about and asked about,in thiscase, sulfurtrioxideandsulfuricacid M(SO3) = 80.1 gmol-l and M(H2SO) = 98.1 gmol-1 Step 2: calculate the amount of sulfuric acid n= M98.1 Step3: use the reaction ratio in the equation to work out the amount of sulfur trioxide needed As the ratio is1:1,the amount is the same,son(SO)=0.765mol Step4:calculate themassof sulfur trioxide m=n×M=0.765×80.1=61.2g"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf034_0197", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE4", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [21, 21], "pdf_pages": [34, 34], "char_count": 336, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE4\n\nThe equationforareaction is:\n\n2NH+HSO4→(NH4)SO4\n\nWhatmassof ammonia isneeded toform100gofammonium sulfate? Step 1:\n\nM(NH3) = 17.0gmol-1 and M(NH4)SO4) = 132.1gmol-1\n\nStep2:\n\nn((NH₄)₂SO₄) = 100 ÷ 132 = 0.757 mol\n\nStep3:\n\nn(NH)=2×0.757=1.51mol (note the 2:1 ratio in the equation)\n\nStep 4:\n\nm(NH)= n×M = 1.51 ×17.0 = 25.7g"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf034_0198", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKINGOUTFORMULAEANDEQUATIONSFROM REACTINGMASSES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [21, 21], "pdf_pages": [34, 34], "char_count": 365, "text": "WORKINGOUTFORMULAEANDEQUATIONSFROM REACTINGMASSES\n\nYou might assume that the formulae and equations for all reactions are alreadyknown.However,there are sometimes two or more possible formulae for a substance. There can alsobe more than one reactionforthesamereactants.Reactingmassescanbeused to identify the correct formula, or which of the reactions is occurring."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf034_0199", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [21, 21], "pdf_pages": [34, 34], "char_count": 290, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3\n\nSodium carbonateexistsasthepure(anhydrous) compoundbutalso as three hydrates. Careful heating can decompose these hydrates tooneof theotherhydratesorto the anhydrouscompound.The measurement of reacting masses can allowyou to determine the correctequationforthedecomposition."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf034_0200", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "Question", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [21, 21], "pdf_pages": [34, 34], "char_count": 223, "text": "Question\n\nA16.7gsampleofahydrateofsodiumcarbonate（Na2CO3.10HO) isheated at a constant temperaturefora specified timeuntil the reaction iscomplete.Amassof3.15gofwaterisobtained.Whatis the equation for the reaction occurring?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf034_0201", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "Method", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [21, 21], "pdf_pages": [34, 34], "char_count": 873, "text": "Method\n\nStep1:calculatethemolarmassesof therelevantsubstances\n\nM(Na₂CO₃·10H₂O) = 286.1 g mol⁻¹ and M(H₂O) = 18.0 g mol⁻¹\n\nStep2:calculatetheamountsof thesesubstances\n\nNa₂CO₃·10H₂O: n = m ÷ M = 16.7 ÷ 286.1 = 0.0584 mol\n\nStep 3: use these amounts to calculate the simplest whole-number ratioforthesesubstances\n\nwater: n = m ÷ M = 3.15 ÷ 18.0 = 0.175 mol\n\nNaCO3.10H2O and H2O are in the ratio 0.0584:0.175 or 1:3 Step 4:use this ratio towork out the equation for thereaction\n\nNaCO3.10HO→NaCO3.7HO+3HO\n\nWe have worked out theNaCO3.7H2O formula by considering the ratiooftheothertwoformulae.\n\nCopperforms two oxides.Both oxides can be converted to copper byheatingwithhydrogen.\n\nQuestion\n\nAn oxide of copper is heated in a stream of hydrogen to constant mass. The masses of copper and water formed are Cu = 17.6g and H2O = 2.56g.What is the equation for the reaction occurring?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf034_0203", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "Method", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [21, 21], "pdf_pages": [34, 34], "char_count": 310, "text": "Method\n\nStep 1: M(Cu) = 63.5 g mol⁻¹ and M(H₂O) = 18.0 g mol⁻¹ Step 2: n(Cu) = 17.6 ÷ 63.5 = 0.277 mol and n(H₂O) = 2.56 ÷ 18.0 = 0.142 mol\n\nStep 3: ratio is 0.277 : 0.142 = 2 : 1\n\nStep4:theequationhas2molofCuand1molof HO,sothe products must be 2Cu +H2O\n\nSo the equation is:\n\nCu2O+H2→2Cu+HOandnot CuO+H2→Cu+H2O"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf034_0204", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [21, 21], "pdf_pages": [34, 34], "char_count": 188, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nOneimportantpartofboththesecalculationmethodsistheuseof therelevantratiofromtheequation.Practisedecidingwhich substancesshouldbeusedfortheratioandwhichwayroundtouse theratio."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf034_0205", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "CHECKPOINT SKILLS ADAPTIVELEARNING", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [21, 21], "pdf_pages": [34, 34], "char_count": 426, "text": "CHECKPOINT SKILLS ADAPTIVELEARNING\n\n• 1.Afertilisermanufacturermakesabatchof20kgof ammonium nitrate.Whatmassofammonia,inkg,doesthemanufacturer needtostartwith?\n\n• 2.A sample ofan oxide of ironwasreduced toironbyheating withhydrogen.Themassofironobtainedwas4.35gandthe mass of water was1.86g.Deduce the equationforthe reaction thatoccurred.\n\nfigAChemistry on this scale needs careful calculations so that no reactantsarewasted."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf034_0206", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [21, 21], "pdf_pages": [34, 34], "char_count": 107, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nhydratecompoundcontainingwaterofcrystallisation,represented byformulaesuch asCuSO4.5HO."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf035_0207", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [22, 22], "pdf_pages": [35, 35], "char_count": 256, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Calculatepercentageyields in laboratory and industrial processes using chemical equations and experimentalresults.\n\nfigA Pharmaceutical companies are always looking for ways to increase the percentage yield when manufacturing a drug."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf035_0208", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "THEORETICALYIELD,ACTUALYIELDANDPERCENTAGEYIELD", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [22, 22], "pdf_pages": [35, 35], "char_count": 660, "text": "THEORETICALYIELD,ACTUALYIELDANDPERCENTAGEYIELD\n\nIn the laboratory, when you are making a product, you naturally want to obtain as much of it as possible from the reactants you start with. In industry, where reactions occur on a much larger scale, and there is economic competition between manufacturers,it is even more important to maximise theproduct of a reaction.\n\nThere are some reasons why the mass of a reaction product may be less than the maximum possible.\n\n• ·Thereactionisreversibleandsomaynotbecomplete.\n\n• Thereareside-reactionsthatlead tootherproductsthat arenotwanted.\n\n• ·The product may need to be purified, which may result in loss of product."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf035_0209", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "TERMINOLOGYRELATINGTOYIELD'", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [22, 22], "pdf_pages": [35, 35], "char_count": 601, "text": "TERMINOLOGYRELATINGTOYIELD'\n\nWe normally use the term‘yield' with other words, such as:\n\n• theoreticalyield\n\n• actual yield\n\n• percentage yield.\n\nIn the laboratory, theoretical yield and actual yield may be measured in grams, but in industry, kilograms and tonnes aremorelikely tobeused.Percentageyield is the termmost oftenused,but you need tounderstand the othertwotermsfirst.\n\nTHEORETICALYIELD\n\nWe calculate theoretical yield using the equation for the reaction, and we use a method you are familiar withfrom previous topics. It is always assumed that the reaction goes to completion,with nolosses."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf035_0211", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [22, 22], "pdf_pages": [35, 35], "char_count": 725, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1\n\nCopper(ll) carbonate is decomposed to obtain copper(ll) oxide. The equation for the reaction is: CuCO→CuO+CO2\n\nWhat is the theoretical yield of copper(ll) oxide obtainable from 5.78g of copper(ll) carbonate?\n\nStep 1: calculate the amount of starting material\n\nn(CuCO₃) = 5.78 ÷ 123.5 = 0.0468 mol\n\nStep 2: use the reacting ratio to calculate the amount of desiredproduct\n\nn(CuO)=0.0468mol\n\nStep 3: calculate the mass of desired product\n\nm = 0.0468 ×79.5 = 3.72g\n\nWORKEDEXAMPLE2\n\nThe equation for the reaction is:\n\n3Mg + 2H₃PO₄ → Mg₃(PO₄)₂ + 3H₂\n\nWhat isthe theoretical yield of magnesiumphosphateobtainablefrom5.62gof magnesium?\n\nStep 1:n(Mg)= 5.62 =0.231mol 24.3\n\n0.0770mol\n\nStep 3: m = 0.0770 ×262.9 = 20.2g"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf036_0213", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "ACTUALYIELD", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [23, 23], "pdf_pages": [36, 36], "char_count": 328, "text": "ACTUALYIELD\n\nThis is the actual mass obtained by weighing the product obtained, not by calculation.\n\nPERCENTAGEYIELD\n\nPercentage yield is calculated using the equation:\n\nactual yield × 100\n\ntheoreticalyield percentage yield\n\nThis calculation may be done independently or in conjunction with the calculation of theoretical yield."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf036_0215", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [23, 23], "pdf_pages": [36, 36], "char_count": 172, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3\n\nThe theoretical yield in a reaction is 26.7tonnes. The actual yield is 18.5 tonnes.What is the percentage yield?\n\n0=69.3% percentage yield = 18.5 x 100 26.7"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf036_0216", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE4", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [23, 23], "pdf_pages": [36, 36], "char_count": 545, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE4\n\nA manufacturer uses this reaction to obtain methanol from carbon monoxide and hydrogen:\n\nCO + 2H₂ → CH₃OH\n\nThemanufacturerobtains4.07tonnesofmethanol startingfrom4.32tonnesof carbonmonoxide What is the percentage yield?\n\nFirst,calculate the theoreticalyield.\n\nStep 1: n(CO) = 4.32 × 10⁶ ÷ 28.0 = 1.54 × 10⁵ mol\n\nStep 2: n(CH;OH) = 1.54 x105 mol (because of 1:1 ratio)\n\nStep 3: m = 1.54 × 105 ×32.0 = 4.94 × 106 mol\n\nThen use that answer to calculate the percentage yield.\n\nPercentage yield = 4.07 × 10⁶ × 100 ÷ 4.94 × 10⁶ = 82.4%"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf036_0217", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [23, 23], "pdf_pages": [36, 36], "char_count": 373, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\nSKILLS\n\nADAPTIVE LEARNING\n\n• 1.A student prepares a sample of copper(ll) sulfate crystals,CuSO4.5HO,weighing7.85g. She started with 4.68g of copper(ll) oxide.What is the percentage yield?\n\n• 2.Amanufacturermakessomeethanoicacidusingthisreaction:\n\nCHOH+CO→CHCOOH\n\nStartingwith50.0kgofmethanol,themanufacturerobtains89.2kgofethanoicacid. Whatisthepercentageyield?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf036_0218", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [23, 23], "pdf_pages": [36, 36], "char_count": 164, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\ntheoreticalyield themaximumpossiblemassof aproductinareaction,assumingcompletereaction and nolosses\n\nactualyieldtheactualmassobtainedinareaction"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf036_0219", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "EXAM HINT", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [23, 23], "pdf_pages": [36, 36], "char_count": 311, "text": "EXAM HINT\n\nYoumaybeaskedinaquestion tosuggestwhyyouhavealow yield.Ifso,you shouldgivea specificexampleinthereaction methodwhereyoumayhave lostproductsuch as,'some solutionwasleftonthefilter paper during filtration'.\n\nLEARNING TIP\n\nForcalculationsusingkilograms andtonnes,rememberthat:\n\n1kg=1x103g\n\n1tonne=1x10°g"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf037_0221", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [24, 24], "pdf_pages": [37, 37], "char_count": 109, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Calculate percentage atom economies using chemical equations and experimental results."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf037_0222", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "BACKGROUNDTOATOMECONOMY", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [24, 24], "pdf_pages": [37, 37], "char_count": 431, "text": "BACKGROUNDTOATOMECONOMY\n\nIn Topic 1C.4, we looked at the percentage yield of a reaction. The closer the value is to 100%, the better. A higher percentage means that less of the starting materials are lost or end up as unwanted products.\n\nindustrialprocess.However,itis not theonly one.Otherfactors include:\n\n• ·the availability or scarcity of non-renewable raw materials\n\n• ·thecostofrawmaterials\n\n• ·the quantity of energy needed."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf037_0223", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "HOWATOMECONOMYWORKS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [24, 24], "pdf_pages": [37, 37], "char_count": 625, "text": "HOWATOMECONOMYWORKS\n\nHereisanexampleofatomeconomyinaction.\n\nThere are two main processes in the manufacture of phosphoric acid. To make the comparison easier to follow, a single summary equation is shown for each process.\n\nProcess 1 Cas(PO4)2 + 3HSO4 →2HPO4 + 3CaSO4\n\nP₄ + 5O₂ + 6H₂O → 4H₃PO₄\n\nThere are advantages and disadvantages of both processes.However, what you can see from these equations is that all of the atoms in the starting materials forProcess 2 end upin the desired product. In Process 1, many of the atoms end up in a second, unwanted product, calcium sulfate.Process 1 has a lower atomeconomythanProcess 2."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf037_0224", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "THECONTRIBUTIONOFBARRYTROST", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [24, 24], "pdf_pages": [37, 37], "char_count": 922, "text": "THECONTRIBUTIONOFBARRYTROST\n\nA chemist from the USA,BarryTrost,developed theidea of atom economy as an alternative way of consider how many atomsfrom the reactants end up in the desired product.\n\nThe expression we use to calculate atom economy (usually described as percentage atom economy) is: molarmass of thedesiredproduct\n\nsum of the molar masses of all products\n\natomeconomy=\n\n×100\n\nYou can see that you do not need a calculator to work out the atom economy of Process 2. There is only one product, so it must be 100%.\n\nFor Process 1, atom economy = (98.0 × 2) × 100 ÷ ((98.0 × 2) + (136.2 × 3)) = 32.4%\n\nSo, you can see that less than one-third of the mass of the starting materials ends up in the desired otherproducthas a use,thenthemanufacturercansellit,whichwouldpartlybalancethelow atom economy of the process. Even if the percentage yield of Process 1 was as high as 100%, the atom economy is still only 32.4%."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf037_0225", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "REACTIONTYPESANDATOMECONOMY", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [24, 24], "pdf_pages": [37, 37], "char_count": 312, "text": "REACTIONTYPESANDATOMECONOMY\n\nWecanmakesomegeneralisations aboutcertaintypesofreaction.\n\n• ·Additionreactionshave100% atomeconomy.\n\n• ·Elimination andsubstitutionreactionshaveloweratomeconomies.\n\n• ·Multistepreactions mayhave even lower atom economies.\n\nfigABarry Trost isa pioneer of the concept of atom economy."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf038_0226", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [25, 25], "pdf_pages": [38, 38], "char_count": 427, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1\n\nSodium carbonate is an important industrial chemical manufactured by the Solvay process. The overall equation for theprocess is:\n\nCaCO₃ + 2NaCl → Na₂CO₃ + CaCl₂\n\nAmanufacturerstartswith75.0kgofcalciumcarbonateandobtains76.5kgofsodiumcarbonate Calculate thepercentageyield and atomeconomyfor thisreaction.\n\nM, values are 100.1 for CaCO3 and 106.0 for Na2CO3. 100.1 79.4 106.0×100 Atom economy= =48.8% 106.0+111.1"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf038_0227", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE2", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [25, 25], "pdf_pages": [38, 38], "char_count": 321, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE2\n\nHydrazine (NH4) can be used as a rocket fuel and is manufactured using this reaction:\n\n2NH₃ + NaOCl → N₂H₄ + NaCl + H₂O\n\nWhat is the atom economyforthisreaction?\n\nYou first need towork out themolar masses of theproducts.These are32.0,58.5 and18.0.\n\nAtom economy = 32.0 × 100 ÷ (32.0 + 58.5 + 18.0) = 29.5%"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf038_0228", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [25, 25], "pdf_pages": [38, 38], "char_count": 586, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3\n\nAmanufacturerofethenewantstoconvertsomeetheneinto1,2-dichloroethane.Heconsiderstwo possiblereactions:\n\nReaction 1 H₂C=CH₂ + Cl₂ → ClCH₂CH₂Cl\n\nReaction 2 2H₂C=CH₂ + 4HCl + O₂ → 2ClCH₂CH₂Cl + 2H₂O\n\nExplain,without doing a calculation,which reaction would be a good choice on the basis of atom economy.\n\nThe answer is Reaction 1, because there is only one product, so allthe atoms in the reactants end up in thedesiredproductand theatomeconomyis100%.\n\nReaction 2 has a lower atom economybecause some of the atoms in the reactants form water,which has no value as a product."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf038_0229", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [25, 25], "pdf_pages": [38, 38], "char_count": 233, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1.Ethanol canbemanufacturedbythe hydrationof ethene:\n\nCH4+HO→C2HsOH\n\nWhatisthe atomeconomyofthisprocess?\n\n• 2.Ethenecanbemanufacturedbythe dehydrationof ethanol:\n\nC₂H₅OH → C₂H₄ + H₂O\n\nWhatisthe atomeconomyofthisprocess?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf038_0230", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [25, 25], "pdf_pages": [38, 38], "char_count": 136, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\natomeconomythemolarmassofthedesiredproductdividedbythesumofthemolarmassesofallthe products,expressed as a percentage"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c3_pdf038_0231", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 3, "chapter_title": "1C Energy", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [25, 25], "pdf_pages": [38, 38], "char_count": 346, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nRememberthat percentage yieldindicateshowefficienta reactionisatconvertingthe reactantsto theproducts. Atomeconomyindicatesthe percentageofatomsfromthe startingmaterialsthatendupin the desired product.\n\nEXAM HINT\n\nAlsonote thatif you havemore than one product,you will need time andenergytoseparatethe desiredproductfromthe mixture."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf039_0233", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [26, 26], "pdf_pages": [39, 39], "char_count": 326, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Knowthetermempiricalformula.\n\n• Useexperimental data tocalculateempirical formulae.\n\n• Ifnecessary,divide the answersfrom this stepbythesmallest of the numbers.\n\n• Thisgivesnumbersthatshouldbein anobviouswholenumber ratio, such as 1: 2 or 3 : 2.\n\n• ·Thesewholenumbers areused towrite theempiricalformula"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf039_0234", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "INTRODUCTIONTOEMPIRICALFORMULAE", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [26, 26], "pdf_pages": [39, 39], "char_count": 649, "text": "INTRODUCTIONTOEMPIRICALFORMULAE\n\nThe three letters 'mol' appear in several words used in chemistry. You will knowsome ofthesefrom yourprevious studies, especially'molecule',which is agroup oftwo or more atoms joined togetherby covalentbonds.Youknowthat amolecule ofwater can be represented by theformula HzO,so this is the molecularformulaofwaterYouhave alreadycomeacrossthe term'relative molecularmass',whichyou will probably remember is 18 (ormore accurately,18.0)for water\n\nThe term'empirical'indicates that some information has been found by experiment. An empirical formula shows the smallest wholenumberratiooftheatomsofeachelementinacompound."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf039_0235", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "ONEEXAMPLEOFANEXPERIMENTALMETHOD", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [26, 26], "pdf_pages": [39, 39], "char_count": 913, "text": "ONEEXAMPLEOFANEXPERIMENTALMETHOD\n\nA simpleexampleinvolvesdetermining theformula of an oxide of copper. This oxide can be converted to copper by removing the oxygen.\n\nHere are the steps in the experiment.\n\n• ·Place a known mass of the oxide ofcopper in the tube.\n\n• Heat the oxide in a stream of hydrogen gas (or natural gas, which is mostly methane).\n\n• The gas reacts with the oxygen in the copper oxide and forms steam.\n\n• ·The colour of the solid gradually changes to orange-brown, which is the colour of copper.\n\n• The excess gas is burned off at the end of the tube for safety reasons.\n\n• After cooling, remove and weigh the solid copper\n\n• It is good practice to heat the solid again in the stream of the gas to check whether its mass changes. Heating to a constant masssuggeststhattheconversiontocopperiscomplete.\n\n• fig AThis apparatus can be used to convert copper oxide to copper by removing the oxygen."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf039_0236", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "CALCULATINGEMPIRICALFORMULAE", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [26, 26], "pdf_pages": [39, 39], "char_count": 875, "text": "CALCULATINGEMPIRICALFORMULAE\n\nThe calculationmethod involves thesesteps.\n\n• ·Divide the mass, or percentage composition by mass, of each element byitsrelative atomic mass.\n\nThe numbersmay not be in an exact ratio because of experimental error, but you should be able to decide what the nearestwhole-number ratio is.\n\nUse at least two significant figures in the calculation (preferably three), and beware of inappropriate rounding. For example, you cannot convert a 1.2: 1.2:1.9 ratio to 1:1:2,because that would giveyou thewronganswers.\n\nIt may help you to organise the calculation using a table, although this is not essential.\n\nCALCULATIONUSINGMASSES\n\nAssume that these are theresults ofthe experiment outlined on theleft.\n\nmass of copper oxide = 4.28g\n\nmass of copper = 3.43g\n\nmass of oxygen removed is 4.28 - 3.43 = 0.85 g\n\nTableA is the calculationtablefor theseresults."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf039_0238", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "CALCULATIONUSINGMASSES", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [26, 26], "pdf_pages": [39, 39], "char_count": 245, "text": "|                    |     Cu |      0 |\n|--------------------|--------|--------|\n| mass of element/ g |   3.43 |   0.85 |\n| relativeatomicmass |   63.5 |   16.0 |\n| division by Ar     | 0.0540 | 0.0531 |\n| ratio              |      1 |      1 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf039_0239", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "tableA", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [26, 26], "pdf_pages": [39, 39], "char_count": 70, "text": "tableA\n\nHere,the ratio is obviously1:1,so the empirical formula is CuO"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf039_0240", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "CALCULATIONUSINGPERCENTAGECOMPOSITIONBYMASS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [26, 26], "pdf_pages": [39, 39], "char_count": 330, "text": "CALCULATIONUSINGPERCENTAGECOMPOSITIONBYMASS\n\nYou do the calculation in the same way, except that you divide percentages instead of masses by the relative atomic masses.\n\nThe calculation table,tableB,refersto a compound containing three elements. The compound has the percentage composition by mass C = 38.4%, H = 4.8%, Cl = 56.8%."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf039_0241", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "CALCULATIONUSINGPERCENTAGECOMPOSITIONBYMASS", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [26, 26], "pdf_pages": [39, 39], "char_count": 257, "text": "|                    |    C |   H |   CI |\n|--------------------|------|-----|------|\n| % of element       | 38.4 | 4.8 | 56.8 |\n| relativeatomicmass | 12.0 | 1.0 | 35.5 |\n| division by Ar     |  3.2 | 4.8 |  1.6 |\n| ratio              |    2 |   3 |    1 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf039_0242", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "tableB", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [26, 26], "pdf_pages": [39, 39], "char_count": 56, "text": "tableB\n\nYou can see that the empirical formula is C2HCl."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf040_0243", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "CALCULATIONWHENTHEOXYGENVALUEISNOTPROVIDED", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [27, 27], "pdf_pages": [40, 40], "char_count": 496, "text": "CALCULATIONWHENTHEOXYGENVALUEISNOTPROVIDED\n\nThe results for some compounds do not include values for oxygen because it is often difficult to obtain an experimental value for the mass ofoxygen.Sometimes you will need toremember to calculate the percentage of oxygen by subtraction.Here is an example.\n\nA compound has the percentage composition by mass Na = 29.1%, S = 40.5%, with the remainder being oxygen.\n\nThe percentage of oxygen = 100 -(29.1 + 40.5) = 30.4%.\n\nTable Cis the calculation table."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf040_0244", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "CALCULATIONWHENTHEOXYGENVALUEISNOTPROVIDED", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [27, 27], "pdf_pages": [40, 40], "char_count": 328, "text": "|                       |   Na |    S |      |\n|-----------------------|------|------|------|\n| %ofelement            | 29.1 | 40.5 | 30.4 |\n| relative atomicmass   | 23.0 | 32.1 | 16.0 |\n| division by Ar        | 1.27 | 1.26 | 1.90 |\n| divisionbythesmallest |    1 |    1 |  1.5 |\n| ratio                 |    2 |    2 |    3 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf040_0245", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "tableC", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [27, 27], "pdf_pages": [40, 40], "char_count": 62, "text": "tableC\n\nYou can now see that the empirical formula is NazS2O3."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf040_0246", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "EXAM HINT", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [27, 27], "pdf_pages": [40, 40], "char_count": 186, "text": "EXAM HINT\n\nTakecaretolookoutfordecimalsthatindicateobviousfractions, e.g.,simplest ratio1:1.33 indicates an empirical formula of 3:4. Howwouldaratioof1:1.25convertintoanempiricalformula?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf040_0247", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "CALCULATIONUSINGCOMBUSTIONANALYSIS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [27, 27], "pdf_pages": [40, 40], "char_count": 1376, "text": "CALCULATIONUSINGCOMBUSTIONANALYSIS\n\nMany organic compounds contain carbon and hydrogen, or carbon,hydrogen andoxygen.When aknown mass ofan organic compound iscompletelyburned,it ispossibletocollect and measurethemassesofcarbondioxideandwaterformed.The calculationismore complexbecausethere areextra steps.Here is an example.\n\nA 1.87g sampleof an organic compound wascompletelyburned, forming 2.65 g of carbon dioxide and 1.63 g of water\n\nIn this type ofcalculation,thefirst steps aretocalculate the masses ofcarbon andhydrogen in thecarbon dioxide andwater\n\n• ·The relative molecular mass of carbon dioxide is 44.0 but, because therelative atomicmass ofcarbonis12.0,the proportion of carbon in carbon dioxide is always 12.0 ÷ 44.0.\n\n• Similarly, the proportion of hydrogen in water is always (2× 1.0)÷18.0.\n\nAll of the carbon in thecarbon dioxide comesfrom thecarbon in the organic compound. Similarly, all of the hydrogen in the water comes from the hydrogen in the organic compound. In this example:\n\nmass of carbon = 2.65 × 12.0 ÷ 44.0 = 0.723 g\n\nmass of hydrogen = 1.63 × 2.0 ÷ 18.0 = 0.181 g\n\nThe original mass of the organic compound was 1.87g, so the difference must be the mass of oxygen present in the organic compound. The mass of oxygen = 1.87 - 0.904 = 0.966g.\n\nTable D is the calculation table.You can see that the empirical formula of the sample compound is CHO."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf040_0248", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "CALCULATIONUSINGCOMBUSTIONANALYSIS", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [27, 27], "pdf_pages": [40, 40], "char_count": 299, "text": "|                     |      C |     H |        |\n|---------------------|--------|-------|--------|\n| mass of element / g |  0.723 | 0.181 |  0.966 |\n| relative atomicmass |   12.0 |   1.0 |   16.0 |\n| division by Ar      | 0.0603 | 0.181 | 0.0604 |\n| ratio               |      1 |     3 |      1 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf040_0249", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "tableD", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [27, 27], "pdf_pages": [40, 40], "char_count": 155, "text": "tableD\n\nFig B shows a model of the glucose molecule.You can count the numbers ofthethreedifferent atomsin themolecule.\n\nfigBA balland-stickmodel ofglucose."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf040_0250", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [27, 27], "pdf_pages": [40, 40], "char_count": 130, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nDividebytherelativeatomicmass,nottheatomicnumberorthe relativemolecularmass.\n\nForoxygen,onlydivideby16.0,notby8or32."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf040_0251", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [27, 27], "pdf_pages": [40, 40], "char_count": 365, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1.A compound has the percentage composition by mass Ca =24.4%,N=17.1%and O=58.5%. Whatisitsempiricalformula?\n\n• 2.Combustion analysis of 2.16g of an organic compound produced 4.33g of carbondioxide and 1.77g of water. Whatisitsempiricalformula?\n\n• 3.Canyouworkouttheempiricalformulaof thismolecule? Blackrepresentscarbon and white represents hydrogen."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf040_0252", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [27, 27], "pdf_pages": [40, 40], "char_count": 97, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nempiricalformula thesmallestwhole-numberratioofatomsofeach elementinacompound"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf041_0253", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [28, 28], "pdf_pages": [41, 41], "char_count": 157, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Knowthetermmolecularformula.\n\n• Useexperimentaldatatocalculatemolecularformulae.\n\n• Use theexpressionpV=nRTforgasesandvolatileliquids."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf041_0254", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "INTRODUCTIONTOMOLECULARFORMULAE", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [28, 28], "pdf_pages": [41, 41], "char_count": 1141, "text": "INTRODUCTIONTOMOLECULARFORMULAE\n\nInTopic1D.1,youlearnedhowtocalculateempiricalformulae.Sometimestheempiricalformula of a compound is the same as its molecular formula. Carbon dioxide (CO2) and water (HzO) are common examples you will know. The molecular formula of a compound shows the actual numbers of the atoms of each element in the compound.\n\nExamplesofcompoundswithdifferentempiricalandmolecularformulaeare:\n\nhydrogen peroxide\n\nempirical formula is HO\n\nmolecular formula is H2O2\n\nbutane\n\nempirical formula is C2H5\n\nmolecular formula is CH10\n\nTo determine the molecular formula of a compound,you need to already know or to calculate:\n\n• ·the empiricalformula\n\n• ·the relative formula mass.\n\nForexample,ifyouhad alreadyfound thattheempiricalformulaof acompoundwasHOandyou then found that its relative formula mass was 34, you could compare the relative mass of the empirical formula(17)with34andworkoutthatthemolecularformulawasdoubletheempiricalformula.\n\nCALCULATINGMOLECULARFORMULAE\n\nYou have already practised calculating an empirical formula from experimental data. Now look at howtocalculateamolecularformulafrom anempiricalformula."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf041_0256", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [28, 28], "pdf_pages": [41, 41], "char_count": 486, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1\n\nIn this example,the empirical formula isgiven.\n\nAcompoundhasthe empirical formulaCH and arelativeformulamassof 104\n\nThe formula mass' of the empirical formula is 13.0.\n\n104 ÷ 13.0 = 8, so the molecular formula of the compound is CgH8\n\nWORKEDEXAMPLE2\n\nIn this example,you first have to work out the empirical formula.A compound contains thepercentage composition by mass Na = 34.3%, C = 17.9%, O = 47.8%, and has a molar mass of 134 gmol-1.\n\nThecalculationsareshownbelow."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf041_0258", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE2", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [28, 28], "pdf_pages": [41, 41], "char_count": 263, "text": "|                    |   Na |    C |      |\n|--------------------|------|------|------|\n| %of element        | 34.3 | 17.9 | 47.8 |\n| relativeatomicmass | 23.0 | 12.0 | 16.0 |\n| division by Ar     | 1.49 | 1.49 | 2.99 |\n| ratio              |    1 |    1 |    2 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf041_0259", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE2", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [28, 28], "pdf_pages": [41, 41], "char_count": 209, "text": "The empirical formula is NaCO2.\n\nThe \"formula mass' of the empirical formula is 23.0 + 12.0 + (2 x 16.0) = 67.0.\n\nThe molarmass,134,is 2x67.0,so the molecularformula of thecompound isNa2C2O4\n\n1.1\n\n1.6\n\n1.8(ii)"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf042_0260", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "THEIDEALGASEQUATIONpV=nRT", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [29, 29], "pdf_pages": [42, 42], "char_count": 360, "text": "THEIDEALGASEQUATIONpV=nRT\n\npV = nRTis the ideal gas equation, and can be used for gases (or volatile liquids above their boiling temperatures) to find the amountofasubstanceinmoles.Ifthemassofthesubstance isalsoknown,thenthemolarmassofthesubstancecanbe calculated. This gives the extra information needed to work out a molecularformulafrom an empiricalformula."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf042_0261", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE4", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [29, 29], "pdf_pages": [42, 42], "char_count": 433, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE4\n\nIn this example,you will calculate the empirical formula,then the amount in moles.After that the molarmass,then the molecular formula. A compoundhas thepercentage compositionbymass C=52.2% H=13.0%,O=34.8%.A sample containing 0.173g of the compound had avolumeof95.0cm3whenmeasured at105kPa and 45°C. Whatisthemolecularformulaofthiscompound?\n\nThe expression can also be rearranged to calculate a value of p, or V or T."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf042_0262", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "SIUNITS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [29, 29], "pdf_pages": [42, 42], "char_count": 553, "text": "SIUNITS\n\nWhen using this equation, you need to be careful that the units are the correct ones.It is always safest to work in SI units.\n\nThe SI units you should use are:\n\n• p=pressure inpascals(Pa)\n\n• V = volume in cubic metres (m3)\n\n• T = temperature in kelvin (K)\n\n• n = amount of substance in moles (mol)\n\n• R = the gas constant-this appears in the Data Booklet providedforuseintheexaminations and has thevalue 8.31Jmol-K-1.\n\nnot SI units.If this is the case,then you will need to convert them toSIunits.TableAshows theconversionsyou arelikely toneed."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf042_0263", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "SIUNITS", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [29, 29], "pdf_pages": [42, 42], "char_count": 325, "text": "tableA\n\n| CONVERSION   | HOWTODOIT                         |\n|--------------|-----------------------------------|\n| kPa→Pa       | multiply by 103                   |\n| cm²→m²       | divide by 106 or multiply by 10-6 |\n| dm² →m3      | divide by 103 or multiply by 10-3 |\n| °℃→K         | add 273                           |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf042_0264", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [29, 29], "pdf_pages": [42, 42], "char_count": 571, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3\n\nIn this example,you will calculate the molar mass of the gas.It may helpyou(atleastuntilyouhavehadmorepractice)towritea listof thevalues with anynecessary conversions.\n\nA 0.280gsampleof agashas a volumeof58.5cm².measured at a pressure of 120kPa and a temperature of 70°℃. Calculate themolar mass of thegas.\n\np = 120 kPa = 120 × 10³ Pa V = 58.5 cm³ = 58.5 × 10⁻⁶ m³ T = 70 °C = 343 K R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ So, n = pV ÷ RT = 120 × 10³ × 58.5 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ (8.31 × 343) = 0.00246 mol M = m ÷ n = 0.280 ÷ 0.00246 = 114 g mol⁻¹\n\nStep1:calculate the empirical formula"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf042_0265", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [29, 29], "pdf_pages": [42, 42], "char_count": 281, "text": "|                      |    C |    H |       |\n|----------------------|------|------|-------|\n| % by mass of element | 52.2 | 13.0 |  34.8 |\n| relativeatomicmass   | 12.0 |  1.0 |  16.0 |\n| division by Ar       | 4.35 | 13.0 | 2.175 |\n| ratio                |    2 |    6 |     1 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf042_0266", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [29, 29], "pdf_pages": [42, 42], "char_count": 447, "text": "Theempirical formula is CHO\n\nStep 2: calculate the amount in moles\n\nn = pV ÷ RT = 105 × 10³ × 95.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ (8.31 × 318) = 0.00377 mol\n\nStep3:calculate themolarmass\n\nM = m ÷ n = 0.173 ÷ 0.00377 = 45.9 g mol⁻¹\n\nStep4:calculatethemolecularformula\n\nThe'formulamass'oftheempirical formula is\n\n(2 × 12.0) + (6 × 1.0) + 16.0 = 46.0\n\nAs 46.0 is the same as the molar mass, then the empirical and molecularformulae arethesame.\n\nThemolecular formula is CHO"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf042_0267", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [29, 29], "pdf_pages": [42, 42], "char_count": 390, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nBecarefulwhenusingtheword'amount.It shouldonlybeusedfor the amount,inmoles,ofa substance.Forexample,The amountof magnesiumusedwas0.150mol'iscorrect.\n\nYoushouldnotuseitinsteadofquantitieswithotherunits.For example,Theamountofmagnesiumusedwas3.6gshouldbeThe mass of magnesium used was3.6g\n\nAnotherexample:\n\nTheamountofwaterusedwas25.0cm3'shouldbeThevolumeof waterusedwas25.0cm3'"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf042_0268", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "CHECKPOINT SKILLS PROBLEMSOLVING", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [29, 29], "pdf_pages": [42, 42], "char_count": 387, "text": "CHECKPOINT SKILLS PROBLEMSOLVING\n\n• 1.A 2.82g sample of a gas has a volume of 1.26 dm, measured at a pressureof103kPa and atemperatureof55°C.Calculate the molarmass of thegas.\n\n• 2.A compound has the percentage composition by mass C = 40.0%, H=6.7%,O=53.3%.A sample containing 0.146g of the compoundhadavolumeof69.5cm3whenmeasuredat98kPa and63°C.What isthemolecularformulaofthiscompound?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c4_pdf042_0269", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 4, "chapter_title": "1D Empirical and molecular formulae", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [29, 29], "pdf_pages": [42, 42], "char_count": 157, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nmolecularformulatheactualnumberofatomsofeachelementin amolecule\n\nmolarmassthemasspermoleofasubstance;ithasthesymbol M and the unitsgmol-1"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf043_0270", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [30, 30], "pdf_pages": [43, 43], "char_count": 131, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Use chemical equations tocalculatereactingvolumes ofgasesandviceversausingtheconceptofmolar volume of gases."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf043_0271", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "MOLARVOLUME", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [30, 30], "pdf_pages": [43, 43], "char_count": 582, "text": "MOLARVOLUME\n\nTheworkofAvogadroandothersled totheidea ofmolarvolume, thevolumeofgasthatcontainsonemoleofthatgas.Themolar volume is approximately the same for all gases, but its value varies with temperature and pressure.The valuemost often used isfor gases atroom temperature andpressure(sometimes abbreviated as rt.p.).Room temperature is 298K(or 25°C) and standard pressure varies,but is often quoted as 1.01× 105Pa.The value of molar volume is usually quoted as 24 dm? mol-1 (equivalent to 24000 cm3). Its symbol is Vm-\n\nVm = 24 dm³ mol⁻¹ at r.t.p.\n\nVm = 24 000 cm?mol-1 at r.t.p."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf043_0272", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "CALCULATIONSINVOLVINGASINGLEGAS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [30, 30], "pdf_pages": [43, 43], "char_count": 819, "text": "CALCULATIONSINVOLVINGASINGLEGAS\n\nIf you are asked about a single gas, the calculation is straight forward. Assume that in these examples, all volumes are measured at r.t.p.\n\nYou need the expressionVm=24 dm²mol-1 which you might want toconsiderusinginthese alternativeforms:\n\nVm = 24.0 = volume in dm³ ÷ amount in mol, or Vm = 24000 = volume in cm³ ÷ amount in mol\n\nYouwill needtorearrange theexpressiondependingonthe actual question. Make sure that you use only 24 and dm3, or only 24 000 and cm3, in the calculation.\n\nEXAMPLE1\n\nWhat is the amount, in moles, of CO in 3.8dm? of carbon monoxide? 3.8 =0.16mol\n\nAnswer = 3.8 ÷ 24 = 0.16 mol\n\nEXAMPLE2\n\nWhat is the amount,in moles,of CO2in 500 cm²of carbon dioxide?\n\n500 Answer= =0.021mol 24000\n\nEXAMPLE3\n\nWhat is the volume of 0.36 mol of hydrogen?\n\nAnswer=24×0.36=8.64dm3"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf043_0276", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "CALCULATIONSINVOLVINGGASESANDSOLIDSORLIQUIDS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [30, 30], "pdf_pages": [43, 43], "char_count": 758, "text": "CALCULATIONSINVOLVINGGASESANDSOLIDSORLIQUIDS\n\nYou may be given a chemical equation that involves one or more gases and a solid or a liquid. If you use information about the\n\n1.8(i)\n\nCP1\n\namount(in mol)of a solid orliquid,you can combine twoof the calculationmethodsthatwehave alreadyused.\n\nThe basis of this type of calculation is thatfor gases you can interconvert between amount and volume.For solids and liquids you can interconvert between amount and mass.\n\n• Step 1: Calculate the amount in moles from either the mass or the volume, depending on which one is given.\n\n• Step2:Use therelevantreactionratioin theequationtocalculate the amount of the other substance.\n\n• Step 3: Convert this amount to a mass or a volume, depending on what thequestion asks."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf043_0277", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "EXAM HINT", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [30, 30], "pdf_pages": [43, 43], "char_count": 219, "text": "EXAM HINT\n\nWhen attemptingmultistepcalculationsinvolving differentunits,itis importanttoshowtheunitsforeachstepofthereaction.Itwillforce you to think aboutwhatyou havejust calculatedrather than leavinga number floating."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf043_0278", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "EXAMPLE4", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [30, 30], "pdf_pages": [43, 43], "char_count": 561, "text": "EXAMPLE4\n\nA piece of magnesium with a mass of 1.00g is added to an excess of dilute hydrochloric acid. What volume of hydrogen gas is formed?\n\nThe equationfor thereaction is:\n\nMg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)\n\nand you are not asked anything about magnesium chloride. You can use the mole expression to calculate the amount of magnesium:\n\nYou can see that the Mg: H ratio in the equation is 1: 1, which means that 0.0412mol of hydrogen is formed.\n\nvolume = 24 × 0.0412 = 0.99 dm³\n\nConvert this amount to avolume andyouhavethe answer: volume=24×0.0412=0.99dm²"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf043_0279", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "EXAMPLE5", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [30, 30], "pdf_pages": [43, 43], "char_count": 672, "text": "EXAMPLE5\n\nCalcium carbonate reacts with nitric acid toform calcium nitrate, water and carbon dioxide, as shown in the equation:\n\nCaCO₃(s) + 2HNO₃(aq) → Ca(NO₃)₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)\n\nIn areaction,100 cm²ofcarbon dioxideis formed.What mass of calciumcarbonateisneededforthis?\n\nYou are not told anything about nitric acid, or asked anything aboutcalciumnitrateorwaterYoucanuse themolar volume expressiontocalculatethe amountofcarbondioxide:\n\namount = 100 ÷ 24000 = 0.00417 mol\n\nYou can see that the CaCO:COratio in the equation is 1:1, which means that 0.00417mol ofcalcium carbonate is needed.\n\nConvert this amounttoamassandyouhavethe answer:\n\nm= n × M=0.00417×100.1=0.42g"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf044_0280", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "EXAMPLE6", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [31, 31], "pdf_pages": [44, 44], "char_count": 746, "text": "EXAMPLE6\n\nAmmoniumsulfatereactswithsodiumhydroxidesolution toformsodium sulfate,water and ammonia,as shown in the equation:\n\n(NH4)SO4(s) + 2NaOH(aq) → Na2SO4(aq) + 2H2O(l) + 2NH(g)\n\nWhat volume of ammonia is formed by reacting 2.16g of ammonium sulfate with excess sodium hydroxidesolution?\n\nYou are not given any information about the sodium hydroxide, and you are not asked anything about sodium sulfate or water You can use the mole expression to calculate the amount of ammonium sulfate:\n\n(NH₄)₂SO₄(s) + 2NaOH(aq) → Na₂SO₄(aq) + 2H₂O(l) + 2NH₃(g)\n\nYou can see that the (NH4)SO4: NH ratio in the equation is 1:2, which means that 0.01635×2=0.0327molofammonia isformed\n\nConvertthisamounttoavolumeandyouhavetheanswer:\n\nvolume=24000×0.0327=785cm²"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf044_0281", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [31, 31], "pdf_pages": [44, 44], "char_count": 116, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nPractiseusingthethree-stepmethodforcalculatingmassesfromvolumes,andvolumesfrommasses,in somereactions."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf044_0282", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [31, 31], "pdf_pages": [44, 44], "char_count": 389, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\nSKILLS\n\nIn these questions, assume that all volumes are measured at r.t.p.\n\n• 1.A flask contains 2dm3 of butane.Whatis the amount,inmoles,ofgasin theflask?\n\n• 2.10.0g of copper(ll) oxide is heated with hydrogen according to this equation:\n\nCuO(s) + H₂(g) → Cu(s) + H₂O(l)\n\nWhat volume of hydrogengas is needed toreact with the copper(ll) oxide,and what mass of copper isformed?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf044_0283", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [31, 31], "pdf_pages": [44, 44], "char_count": 124, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nmolarvolume thevolume occupiedby1mol of anygas;this isnormally 24 dmor24000 cm²at r.t.p.\n\nPROBLEMSOLVING"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf045_0284", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [32, 32], "pdf_pages": [45, 45], "char_count": 86, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Calculate the concentration of a solution, in gdm-3 andmoldm-3."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf045_0285", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "CALCULATIONSUSINGMASSCONCENTRATION (c-up 6)", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [32, 32], "pdf_pages": [45, 45], "char_count": 708, "text": "CALCULATIONSUSINGMASSCONCENTRATION (c-up 6)\n\nIfyouknow themassofasolute thatyoudissolveinasolvent (usuallywater),and thevolume of thesolutionformed,thenit is straightforward to calculate the mass concentration.\n\nYou use the expression:\n\nmass concentration in g dm⁻³ = mass of solute in g ÷ volume of solution in dm³\n\nIn this topic, we only use values based on g and dm?. You may sometimes see other units, such as g cm-3 and kgm-3.\n\nAs with other similar expressions, you will need to rearrange it, depending on the wording of the question.You also need to remember to convert cm? to dm3 (by dividingby 1000).\n\nEXAMPLE1\n\n200 cm3 of a solution contains 5.68g of sodium bromide. What is its mass concentration?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf045_0287", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "CALCULATIONSUSINGMOLARCONCENTRATION(moldm-3)", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [32, 32], "pdf_pages": [45, 45], "char_count": 787, "text": "CALCULATIONSUSINGMOLARCONCENTRATION(moldm-3)\n\nMolar concentration(it used tobe called molarity) is used more often than mass concentration. If only the term concentration'is mentioned,thenyou should assume thatit refers to molar concentration.\n\nTheunits ofmolar concentration aremoldm-3,and this is often denoted by using square brackets. If a solution of hydrochloric acid has a concentration of 0.150moldm-3,this canbe shown as [HCl] = 0.150 mol dm-3. The symbol c is sometimes used to represent molar concentration.\n\nYouneedtobeabletousethesetwoexpressionstogether:\n\namount = mass ÷ molar mass, or n = m/M\n\nand\n\n(molar) concentration = amount ÷ volume, or c = n/V\n\nAs previously, you may need to rearrange these expressions. Look at the question wording to decidewhich one tousefirst."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf045_0288", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [32, 32], "pdf_pages": [45, 45], "char_count": 274, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1\n\nAchemistmakes5o0cm3ofasolutionofnitricacidofconcentration 0.800moldm-3.What massof HNOdoes she need?\n\nStep 1: You are given values of V and c, so you can use the second expressiontocalculateavalueforn.\n\nmass concentration = m ÷ V = 5.68 ÷ 0.200 = 28.4 g dm⁻³"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf045_0289", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "EXAMPLE2", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [32, 32], "pdf_pages": [45, 45], "char_count": 310, "text": "EXAMPLE2\n\nThe concentration of a solution is 15.7gdm-3. What mass of solute is there in750cm²of solution?\n\nm = mass concentration × V = 15.7 × 0.750 = 11.8 g\n\nn = c × V = 0.800 × 0.500 = 0.400 mol\n\nStep2:You can now use thefirst expression to calculate themassof nitric acid.\n\nm = n × M = 0.400 × 63.0 = 25.2 g"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf045_0290", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "EXAMPLE3", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [32, 32], "pdf_pages": [45, 45], "char_count": 734, "text": "EXAMPLE3\n\nAchemistuses280gofa solutetomakeasolution of concentration28.4gdm-3.What volume of solution doeshemake?\n\nV = m ÷ mass concentration = 280 ÷ 28.4 = 9.86 dm³\n\nfigAThesecontainersindicateincreasingrelative solute concentrations by increasing colour intensity. Unfortunately, most solutions we use are notcoloured,sowecannotrelyondifferentcolourintensitiestoindicate differentconcentrations.\n\nA studenthas50.0gofsodium chloride.Whatvolume ofa 0.450moldm-3solutioncanhemake?\n\nStep 1: You are given the value of m and can work out M from the PeriodicTable,soyoucancalculate n.\n\nn = m ÷ M = 50.0 ÷ 58.5 = 0.855 mol\n\nStep2:You can now use thesecond expression to calculate the volumeofsolution.\n\nV = n ÷ c = 0.855 ÷ 0.450 = 1.90 dm³"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf045_0291", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "CALCULATIONSFROMEQUATIONSUSINGCONCENTRATION ANDMASS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [32, 32], "pdf_pages": [45, 45], "char_count": 315, "text": "CALCULATIONSFROMEQUATIONSUSINGCONCENTRATION ANDMASS\n\nIn this type of calculation, you can use an equation to calculate molarconcentration of anothersubstance,andviceversa.\n\nThe expressions you need are the same as those you have just used,butyoualsoneedtheequationforthereactionsothatyou can see the reacting ratio."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf046_0292", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [33, 33], "pdf_pages": [46, 46], "char_count": 456, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3\n\nAn excess of magnesium is added to 100 cm3 of 1.50 mol dm-3 hydrochloric acid. The equation for the reaction is:\n\nMg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂\n\nWhat mass of hydrogen is formed?\n\nStep1:You aregiven thevalues of V and c,soyou can use thesecond expressiontocalculate thevalue of n forhydrochloricacid.\n\nn =0.100x1.50=0.150mol\n\nStep 2: The ratio for HCl:H is 2: 1, so n(H2) = 0.150 ÷ 2 = 0.0750mol\n\nStep 3: For hydrogen, m = n xM = 0.0750x2.0 = 0.15g"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf046_0293", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE4", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [33, 33], "pdf_pages": [46, 46], "char_count": 406, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE4\n\nAmassof47.8gofmagnesiumcarbonatereactswith2.50moldm-3hydrochloricacid.Theequationfor thereaction is:\n\nMgCO₃ + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂\n\nWhatvolumeofacidisneeded?\n\nStep1:You aregiven thevalue of m and can work out Mfrom thePeriodicTable,soyou can calculate n.\n\nn = 47.8 ÷ 84.3 = 0.567 mol\n\nStep 2: The ratio for MgCO3: HCl is 1: 2,so n(HCI) = 2×0.567 = 1.134mol\n\nV = 1.134 ÷ 2.50 = 0.454 dm³"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf046_0294", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [33, 33], "pdf_pages": [46, 46], "char_count": 166, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nRememberthatthevolumesreferredtointhistopicareofsolutions,notsolvents.lfyoudissolveasolutein 100cm²ofasolvent,thevolumeof thesolutionisnotexactly100cm3."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf046_0295", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [33, 33], "pdf_pages": [46, 46], "char_count": 369, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\nSKILLS\n\nPROBLEM SOLVING\n\n• 1.50.0g of sodiumhydroxideis dissolved in water to make1.50dm²of solution.What is themolar concentrationofthesolution?\n\n• 2.150cmof 0.125moldm-3 lead(ll)nitratesolution ismixed with an excess ofpotassiumiodidesolution. Theequationforthereactionthatoccursis:\n\nPb(NO)(aq)+2KI(aq)→Pbl2(s)+2KNO3(aq)\n\nWhatmassoflead(ll)iodideisformed?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf046_0296", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [33, 33], "pdf_pages": [46, 46], "char_count": 306, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nsoluteasubstance thatisdissolved\n\nsolventasubstancethatdissolvesasolute\n\nsolutionasolutedissolvedinasolution\n\nmassconcentration(ofasolution)themass(ing)ofthesolutedividedbythevolumeofthesolution molarconcentration(ofasolution)theamount(inmol)ofthesolutedividedbythevolumeof the solution"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf047_0297", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [34, 34], "pdf_pages": [47, 47], "char_count": 95, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\nUnderstand the term parts per million (ppm), e.g. gases in the atmosphere."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf047_0298", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "CONCENTRATIONSOFSOLUTIONS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [34, 34], "pdf_pages": [47, 47], "char_count": 456, "text": "CONCENTRATIONSOFSOLUTIONS\n\nConcentrations ofsolutions can alsobecomparedusingtheterm parts per million, or ppm. It is often used for pollutants in water.\n\nThink of theword'percent'-the'cent'part refers to100,so50% means 50 out of 100, and parts per million means out of a million. 50 ppm means 50 parts out of 1000000 parts. The'parts'usually refer to mass. The parts can be measured in any unit of mass, but grams or milligrams are the most commonly used."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf047_0299", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "CALCULATIONSFORSOLUTIONSINPPM", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [34, 34], "pdf_pages": [47, 47], "char_count": 355, "text": "CALCULATIONSFORSOLUTIONSINPPM\n\nA concentration of 1ppm means1g in 1000000g,or1mg in 1000000mg.\n\nThis expression canbeused to calculateconcentrations inppm:\n\nconcentration in ppm = mass of solute × 1 000 000 ÷ mass of solvent\n\nThe masses can be in any units,but they must be the same units. If different units are given, then one of them must be converted."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf047_0300", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [34, 34], "pdf_pages": [47, 47], "char_count": 333, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE1\n\nA solutioncontains 0.176gof solute dissolved in750gof solvent. What is theconcentration inppm?\n\nAstheunitsofsoluteandsolventarethesame,thevaluescanbe directlyinserted into the expression.\n\nmass of solutex1000000\n\nconcentration in ppm = mass of solute × 1 000 000 ÷ mass of solvent\n\n= 0.176 × 1 000 000 ÷ 750 = 235 ppm"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf047_0301", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE2", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [34, 34], "pdf_pages": [47, 47], "char_count": 608, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE2\n\nAmassof 23mgof sodiumchlorideisdissolved in900gof water. What is theconcentration of sodiumchloride in thesolution inppm?\n\nAs theunitsofsoluteandsolvent aredifferent,either themassof soluteorthemassofsolventmustbeconvertedso theyarethe same.Then thevaluescanbe directly inserted into theexpression.\n\nFirst, convert the mass of sodium chloride from mg to g (divide by 1000): mass of sodium chloride = 23 ÷ 1000 = 0.023 g\n\nmassof solutex1000000\n\nmass of sodium chloride = 23 ÷ 1000 = 0.023 g concentration in ppm = mass of solute × 1 000 000 ÷ mass of solvent = 0.023 × 1 000 000 ÷ 900 = 26 ppm"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf047_0302", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [34, 34], "pdf_pages": [47, 47], "char_count": 326, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE3\n\nAsampleofriverwatercontainsphosphateionswitha concentrationof17ppm.Whatis themassofphosphateionsin 500gof theriverwater?\n\nIn this example,the expressionneedstoberearranged:\n\nconcentrationinppmxmassof solvent\n\nmass of solute = concentration in ppm × mass of solvent ÷ 1 000 000 = 17 × 500 ÷ 1 000 000 = 0.0085 g"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf047_0303", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "GASESINTHEATMOSPHERE", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [34, 34], "pdf_pages": [47, 47], "char_count": 974, "text": "GASESINTHEATMOSPHERE\n\nMaunaLoa is the name of avolcano in Hawai.It is also the locationofaweatherobservatorythathasrecordedthelevelsof carbondioxidein the atmosphereover alongperiod oftime.\n\nFigA showshow thelevelsofcarbondioxideatMaunaLoahave changed overaperiod of50years.There aresmallvariations during each year. In 1960, the level was about 316 ppm. By 2010, the level had reached nearly 390 ppm.Since 2015 it has been over 400 ppm, and many scientists believe that it will always remain above this value.\n\nYear\n\nfig AThisgraph shows the steadyincrease in atmospheric carbon dioxide over five decades.\n\nThe concentrations of pollutant gases in the atmosphere are often given as values in ppm. Instead of using masses, the comparison is usually by volume, so calculations are done in a different way. Sometimes the values are quoted in ppmv- the v shows that the valuereferstoconcentrationbyvolume\n\nA concentration of 1 ppmv means 1cm² in 1000000 cm3, or 1 cm²in1000dm3."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf048_0304", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "TOPIC 1", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [35, 35], "pdf_pages": [48, 48], "char_count": 544, "text": "TOPIC 1\n\nResidents ofDhaka,the capital city of Bangladesh,are concerned about air quality. As in many cities, road traffic leads to high levels of carbon monoxide, especially during rush hours, when many people are travelling to and from work. Levels of CO in the air can be as high as100 ppm. Outside the city there are many factories producing bricks for constructing buildings. These factories are responsibleformanypollutants,includingsulfur dioxide and particulate matter\n\nfig BThis brickfactory releasespollutant gasesinto the atmosphere."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf048_0305", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "CALCULATIONSFORGASESINPPM", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [35, 35], "pdf_pages": [48, 48], "char_count": 288, "text": "CALCULATIONSFORGASESINPPM\n\nThis expression can be used to calculate concentrations in ppm.\n\nconcentration in ppm = volume of gas × 1 000 000 ÷ volume of air\n\nThe volumes can be in any units,but they must be the same units. If different units are given, then one of them must be converted."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf048_0306", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE6", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [35, 35], "pdf_pages": [48, 48], "char_count": 306, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE6\n\nTwo samples of air containing sulfur dioxidewere analysed.The resultsforSample1showedthat500dm3ofaircontained37cm3 ofsulfurdioxide.TheresultsforSample2showedthattherewere 1.4dm3of sulfur dioxide in4000dm3of air.Show,by calculation, which sample has the higher concentration,in ppm,of sulfur"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf048_0307", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "Sample1", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [35, 35], "pdf_pages": [48, 48], "char_count": 198, "text": "Sample1\n\nSample2\n\nconcentration in ppm =\n\n= 37 ÷ 1000 × 1 000 000 ÷ 500 = 74 ppm\n\nvolume of gas x 1 000 000\n\nvolume of air\n\n= 1.4 × 1 000 000 ÷ 4000 = 350 ppm\n\nSample 2 has the higher concentration."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf048_0308", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [35, 35], "pdf_pages": [48, 48], "char_count": 144, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nForppmcalculationswith solutions,use the expressioninvolving masses.For calculationswithgases,use the expressioninvolving volumes."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf048_0309", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [35, 35], "pdf_pages": [48, 48], "char_count": 197, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1.0.2gofpotassiumsulfateisdissolvedinwatertomake800gof solution.What istheconcentrationof thesaltinppm?\n\n• 2.200dmofaircontains58cm3ofchlorine.Whatisthe concentrationof chlorineinppm?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf048_0310", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [35, 35], "pdf_pages": [48, 48], "char_count": 581, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nSome nitrogen dioxidegas,with avolumeof1.5dm²,mixeswith 10000 dm3of air.Whatis the concentration of nitrogendioxide,in ppm,in the air?\n\nAs thevolumeunitsof bothgases are the same,then thevaluescan be directly inserted into the expression.\n\nconcentration in ppm = volume of gas × 1 000 000 ÷ volume of air\n\n= 1.5 × 1 000 000 ÷ 10 000 = 150 ppm\n\nparts per million(ppm) the number of parts of one substancein one millionpartsofanothersubstance;ameasureusedtodescribe chemical concentration;usually,'parts'referstomassesofboth substances,ortovolumesofbothsubstances"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c5_pdf048_0311", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 5, "chapter_title": "1E Calculations with solutions and gases", "section": "WORKEDEXAMPLE5", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [35, 35], "pdf_pages": [48, 48], "char_count": 274, "text": "WORKEDEXAMPLE5\n\n5000 dm²ofairisfoundtocontainozonewithaconcentrationof 87ppm.What volume of ozone is in this sample of air? In this example,the expressionneeds toberearranged:\n\nconcentration in ppmxvolume of air\n\nvolume of gas =\n\n1000000\n\n= 87 × 5000 ÷ 1 000 000 = 0.435 dm³"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf049_0312", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "COINSINHISTORY", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [36, 36], "pdf_pages": [49, 49], "char_count": 184, "text": "COINSINHISTORY\n\nThe chemical analysis of coinagefrom earlier periods in history can offer insights into the technology of metal extraction available at the'Chemistry in detail'section."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf049_0313", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "ALL THAT GLISTENSIS NOT GOLD", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [36, 36], "pdf_pages": [49, 49], "char_count": 1366, "text": "ALL THAT GLISTENSIS NOT GOLD\n\nfig A Trajan Decius,249-251 BCE. Denomination: Silver Antoninianus. Mint:Rome.\n\nOver the centuries,the base metals Fe,Cu,Ni,Zn,Al,Sn and Pb have been used as minor alloy constituents or as principal componentsin coins.Generally,a metalmustbereasonably hard-wearing to ensure the economic lifetime of a coin, and must And it must notbe too expensive.As a soft and expensive metal, gold is now usually alloyed with copper to give a hard-wearing alloy.Britishgoldsovereigns,whicharestillminted,aremade from 2 carats of alloy and 22 carats of gold. They comprise 91% Au and, 8.3% Cu. (A carat is a measure of the purity of gold, with pure gold being 24 carat.) Bronze (Cu-Sn alloy) farthings issued between1897 and1917were darkened using Na2S2O3,resulting in a surface layer of copper sulfide.This was done to avoid confusion between a newlymintedfarthing and agoldhalf-sovereign.The half-sovereign being worth 480 times as much as the farthing. Bronzepennies issuedbetween1944 and1946weresimilarly treatedtodeterthehoardingofnewpenniesattheendofthe SecondWorldWar.\n\ncentury,at a timewhen platinumhadrecentlybeenfound inthe Ural Mountains.Forawhile,thevalue of platinumfellbelow that of gold,and this gave rise to the emergence of counterfeit sovereignsinthe1870s.Theseweremadefromplatinumandhad a thinlayerofelectrochemicallydepositedgold."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf049_0314", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "ALL THAT GLISTENSIS NOT GOLD", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [36, 36], "pdf_pages": [49, 49], "char_count": 1353, "text": "Silver coinage was the universal medium of tradingfor centuries. The silver content of the coinsmadein citymintswas a reflection of the status of the city as a trading centre.Traders of good repute only dealt in high quality silver coins, as evidenced by the high silvercontentof thosecoinsfound alongtheoldestablished trading routes,e.g. from the Mediterranean, through the Middle East and Central Asia,to China.\n\nPlatinum made only a brief appearance as a coinage metal. A fewhigh-value coinsinRussiaweremadeinthemid-nineteenth If military expenditure increased,or during times of declining prosperity, a government would put only enough silver in its coinsfor them toremain acceptableto thepublic.Sometimes, however,therewasamoresubtlereasonforthedeclineinsilver content.Up to circa 100BCE,Roman silver coins contained more than90%Ag.Thereafter,silversuppliesgrewscarcer, and Roman technology was unable to extract silver from lowgrade ores,which by the third century BCEwere the only primary sources of silver available. However, at around this time,deposits ofAgCl(chlorargynite)werefound in Cornwall, Brittany and Alsace.The Romans observed that if copper or bronze coins were dipped in molten AgCl (mp 455°C), they becamecoatedwithsilver:\n\nCu+2AgCl→2Ag+CuCl2\n\nFrom an articleinEducationinChemistry magazine,publishedby theRoyalSocietyof Chemistry"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf049_0315", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "DID YOU KNOW?", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [36, 36], "pdf_pages": [49, 49], "char_count": 399, "text": "DID YOU KNOW?\n\nInApril 1940duringtheNazioccupationofDenmark,theHungarian-bornchemistGeorgedeHevesydecidedtohidetheNobelPrizemedals ofcolleaguesMaxvonLaueandJamesFranckbydissolvingtheminaquaregia(amixtureofconcentratednitricandhydrochloricacid). Theresultingsolutionsatonashelfforthenext5yearsattractingnoparticularattention.Attheendofthewar,thegoldwasrecovered fromthesolution andthetwomedalsrecast."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf050_0316", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "SCIENCE COMMUNICATION", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [37, 37], "pdf_pages": [50, 50], "char_count": 352, "text": "SCIENCE COMMUNICATION\n\n• 1The articleis about the development of a coin-based currency from the earliest times.Answer the following three questions and explain your answers with reasons or examples. In what ways is an article of this type different from a research paper?Which audience is this article aimed at? Is there any bias present in the report?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf050_0317", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "CHEMISTRY IN DETAIL", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [37, 37], "pdf_pages": [50, 50], "char_count": 1821, "text": "CHEMISTRY IN DETAIL\n\n• 2. (a) Modern gold sovereigns weigh 7.99 g (to two decimal places). Using the information in the article, calculate the number of moles of gold in a sovereign.\n\n• (b) Calculate the percentage of gold in an 18 carat ring.\n\n• 3. (a) According to the reaction between copper and silver chloride in the article how many moles ofsilverareproducedfrom1moleofcopper?\n\n• (b)Calculate the number of moles of copper in 2.17g (Ar Cu = 63.5)\n\n• (c)When 2.17g of copper reacts with excess silver chloride according to the equation a total of 5.40gof silver isrecovered.Assuming that all of the copperreactscalculate thepercentage yieldof the silverrecovered.(ArAg=107.9)\n\n• 4. Reread the third paragraph of the article. Using the information given, write half-equations for\n\n• (i)thereductionof thiosulfateionstosulfidein acidicsolution,and\n\n• (i) the oxidation of copper metal to copper(ll) ions.\n\n• 5. A gold sovereign is analysed using the following chemical procedure.\n\n• The sovereign is reacted with excess concentrated nitric acid in a fume cupboard.\n\n• 2 The resultant mixture is filtered to remove the unreacted gold.\n\n• 3 The resultant solution is made up in distilled water to a volume of 250 cm3\n\n• 4 A volume of 25 cm3 of this solution is pipetted into a conical flask and 50 cm3 of 0.1 mol dm-3 Kl(aq) is added (this is an excess). The resultant solution immediately turns an orange-brown colour.\n\n• This solution is then titrated with 0.050 mol dm-3 Na2S2Os(aq) solution until the solution fades to a strawyellow colour.\n\n• 6 Starch indicatoris then addedtogive ablue-blackcolour.\n\n• 7 Further Na2S2O3(aq) solution is added until the solution becomes colourless.\n\n• 8 The first titration volume is recorded.\n\n• 6 The titration is repeated 3 times and the results recorded to the nearest 0.05 cm3."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf050_0318", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "CHEMISTRY IN DETAIL", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [37, 37], "pdf_pages": [50, 50], "char_count": 269, "text": "| Titre number      |     1 |     2 |     3 |     4 |\n|-------------------|-------|-------|-------|-------|\n| Finalvolume/cm3   | 26.00 | 26.35 | 25.90 | 25.85 |\n| Initialvolume/cm3 |  0.00 |  0.55 |  0.00 |  0.00 |\n| Titre/ cm3        | 26.00 |       |       |       |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf050_0319", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "Results table", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [37, 37], "pdf_pages": [50, 50], "char_count": 982, "text": "Results table\n\n• (a)Suggest why step 1 must be done in a fume cupboard.\n\n• (b) Complete the results table and calculate a suitable mean titre.\n\n• (c) Calculate the percentage uncertainty in the reading for titre number 3.\n\n• (d)Match the equationsbelow with the reactions takingplace in the method described. Cu(s)+4H+(aq)+2NO(aq)→Cu²+(aq)+2NO2(g)+2H2O(1) 2Cu²+(aq)+41-(aq)→→2Cul(s)+12（aq) 2SO-(aq)+12(aq)→21-(aq)+SO-(aq)\n\n• (e) Using appropriate calculations, decide whether the sovereign is genuine or not.\n\n• (f)What assumptions have been made in the procedure?\n\nTHINKING BIGGER TIP\n\nIn your previous study of chemistry, you will have coveredmetalsandtheir methods ofextraction.This wouldbeagoodchancefor you toreview some of that work.Considerhowmetal reactivity relatesto methods ofextraction.\n\nINTERPRETATIONNOTE\n\nSeeifyoucanfindasimilar articleonthedevelopment of pigments and dyes over the ages or the development of textiles.Considerhow thethe chemicalideasare represented."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf050_0322", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "ACTIVITY", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [37, 37], "pdf_pages": [50, 50], "char_count": 359, "text": "ACTIVITY\n\nThe history of human technology is closely associatedwith metals.\n\nDraw a timeline identifying the metals used and the culturesresponsiblefor developing the technology.\n\n• ·You should consider the following metals:gold,silver, copper, iron, aluminium, titanium and uranium.\n\n• ·Consideralso themethods of extraction and the key usages of the metals."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf051_0323", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "EXAM PRACTICE", "content_type": "chapter_questions", "book_pages": [38, 38], "pdf_pages": [51, 51], "char_count": 390, "text": "EXAM PRACTICE\n\n• 1How many molecules of oxygen are there in a 1.00 g sample of O2?\n\n(Avogadro constant, L = 6.02 × 1023 mol-1)\n\n• A1.88× 1022\n\n• B 3.76 × 1022\n\n• C 9.63×1024\n\n• D 1.93 × 1025\n\n[1]\n\n(Total for Question 1 = 1 mark)\n\n• 2Whatistheconcentration,inmoldm-3,ofasolutionofsodium chloride containing 4.27 g of NaCl in 300 cm3 of solution?\n\n• A0.0219\n\n• B0.243\n\n• C 4.11\n\n• D 45.7\n\n[1]"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf051_0324", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "(Total for Question 2=1mark)", "content_type": "chapter_questions", "book_pages": [38, 38], "pdf_pages": [51, 51], "char_count": 1759, "text": "(Total for Question 2=1mark)\n\n• 3The overall equation for one method of manufacturing hydrogenfrom methane is\n\nCH4+2H2O→4H2+CO\n\nIn one batch, 5.12 tonnes of hydrogen were obtained from 13.6 tonnes of methane.\n\nWhat is the percentage yield of hydrogen for this batch?\n\n• A37.6\n\n• B 42.5\n\n• C 54.4\n\n• D 75.3\n\n[1]\n\n(Total for Question 3 = 1 mark)\n\n• 4The equation for a reaction that can be used tomanufacture lithium carbonate is\n\n2Li2O2+2CO2→2LiCO+O2\n\nWhat is the atom economy for this reaction?\n\n• A58.9\n\n• B 62.1\n\n• 6The equation for a precipitation reaction is\n\nAgNOs(aq) + NaCl(aq) →AgCl(s) + NaNOs(aq)\n\nA solutioncontaining0.04molsilvernitrateisadded toa solution containing 0.06 mol sodium chloride.\n\nWhat mass of precipitate forms?\n\n• A 5.74g\n\n• C 8.60g\n\n• B 7.17g\n\n• D 14.3g\n\n[1]\n\n(Total for Question 6=1mark)\n\n• 7Magnesiumchloridemaybepreparedbyreactingmagnesium with dilute hydrochloric acid.\n\nA sample of magnesium of mass 1.215 g was added to60.0cm²dilutehydrochloricacidofconcentration 2.00 mol dm-3.\n\nThe equation for the reaction is\n\nMg(s) + 2HCl(aq)→ MgCl2(aq) +H2(g)\n\n• (a)State two observations that are madewhenmagnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid.\n\n• (b) (i) Calculate the amount, in moles, of magnesium used.\n\n• (i)Calculate the amount, in moles, of hydrochloric acid used.\n\n• (ii) Use your answers to (b)(i) and (b)(i)toshow that the hydrochloric acid is in excess.\n\n[2]\n\n[1]\n\n[2]\n\n• (c) Calculate the volume, measured at rt.p. of hydrogen produced in this reaction.\n\n• (The molar volume of hydrogen at rt.p. is 24 dm? mol-1) [2]\n\n(Total for Question 7=8marks)\n\n• 8Ammoniumnitrate(NHNOs)canbepreparedby thereaction of ammonia with nitric acid.\n\n• The equation for thereaction is\n\nNH(aq) +HNOs(aq) →NH4NOs(aq)\n\n• C 69.8\n\n• D 82.2"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf051_0325", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "[1] (Total for Question 4 = 1 mark)", "content_type": "chapter_questions", "book_pages": [38, 39], "pdf_pages": [51, 52], "char_count": 1060, "text": "[1] (Total for Question 4 = 1 mark)\n\n• 5The equation for a displacement reaction is Zn + FeSO4→Fe + ZnSO4\n\nWhich species is displaced in this reaction?\n\n• A zinc\n\n• B iron\n\n• C sulfur\n\n• D oxygen\n\n[1]\n\n• (a)(i)Calculate the minimum volume of nitric acid, of concentration 0.500 moldm-3,that is required to react completely with 25.0 cm? of aqueous ammonia of concentration 2.00 mol dm-3.\n\n[2]\n\n• (i)Howcould asolidsampleofammoniumnitratebe obtainedfromthereactionmixture?\n\n• (b)When ammonium nitrate is heated,it decomposes according to the following equation:\n\nNHNOs(S) → N2O(g) + 2H2O(l)\n\nA 4.00 g sample of ammonium nitrate was carefully heated to produce only NzO and water.\n\n[1]\n\n(Total for Question 5 = 1 mark)\n\n• (i)Calculate the amount, in moles, of ammonium nitrate used.\n\n[2]\n\n• (i)Calculate the volume of NzO that was formed. [1]\n\n(Assume that the molar volume of NzO is 24 dm?mol-1 under the experimental conditions.)\n\n• (i) NzO can be decomposed into its elements by further heating. Write an equation for this reaction. Include statesymbols.\n\n[2]"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf052_0326", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "（TotalforQuestion8=8marks)", "content_type": "chapter_questions", "book_pages": [39, 39], "pdf_pages": [52, 52], "char_count": 681, "text": "（TotalforQuestion8=8marks)\n\n• 9Phosphorusforms threechloridesofmolecularformulae PCl, PCls and PCl4.\n\n• (a)State what is meant by molecular formula.\n\n[1]\n\n• (b)PCls can be prepared by reacting white phosphorus (P4) with chlorinegas.\n\n• Write an equation for this reaction.State symbols are not required.\n\n[1]\n\n• (c) Solid PCls reacts vigorously with water to form a solution containing a mixture of two acids,HPOand HCl.\n\n• Write an equation for this reaction.Include state symbols. [2]\n\n• (d)A compound was found to have the following percentage composition by mass:\n\nP=30.39%;Cl=69.61%\n\n• (i) Use this information to identify the chloride. [3]\n\nTheresults are shown in the table."}
