{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf052_0328", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "（TotalforQuestion8=8marks)", "content_type": "chapter_questions", "book_pages": [39, 39], "pdf_pages": [52, 52], "char_count": 376, "text": "• (a) (i) Use the results to calculate the molar mass of the metal.\n\nAssume the molar volume of hydrogen is 24.0dm?mol-1under the experimental conditions. [3]\n\n• (i)Give the most likely identity of the metal. Justify your answer.\n\n[2]\n\n• (b) (i)Identity the major procedural error in the experiment.\n\n[1]\n\n• (i)State a modification that would reduce this procedural error\n\n[1]"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf052_0329", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "(Total for Question 10 = 7marks)", "content_type": "chapter_questions", "book_pages": [39, 39], "pdf_pages": [52, 52], "char_count": 472, "text": "(Total for Question 10 = 7marks)\n\n• 11Azides are compounds ofmetalswith nitrogen.Some azides are used as detonators in explosives.However,sodium azide (NaNs) is used in air bags in cars.\n\n• (a) Sodium azide decomposes into its elements when heated. 2NaN(s) →→ 2Na(l) + 3N(g)\n\nWhat is the volume of gas produced, measured at r.t.p., whenonemoleofsodium azide isdecomposed?\n\n• A 24 dm3\n\n• B 36 dm²\n\n• C 48dm3\n\n• (i)Give the systematic name for this chloride. [1]\n\n• D 72 dm²"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u1_c0_pdf052_0330", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 1, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 1: Formulae, Equations and Amount of Substance", "section": "(TotalforQuestion9=8marks)", "content_type": "chapter_questions", "book_pages": [39, 39], "pdf_pages": [52, 52], "char_count": 1079, "text": "(TotalforQuestion9=8marks)\n\n• 10The diagram shows the apparatus used to collect and measure the volume of hydrogen given off when a sample of a Group2metalreactswith dilutehydrochloric acid.\n\nThe equationfor thereaction is\n\nM(s) + 2HCl(aq)→MCl2(aq) + H2(g)\n\nwhereMis thesymbolfor theGroup2metal.\n\nThe apparatus was set up as shown in the diagram.\n\nA sample of the metal was weighed and then placed into the conical flask.\n\nThe bung was removed and an excess of acid was then added to the metal. The bung was replaced.\n\nWhen thereaction wascomplete,and thegascollected had cooled to room temperature, the volume of gas collected in the measuring cylinder was measured.\n\n(Assume one mole of gas occupies 24 dm? at rt.p.) [1]\n\n• (b)(i)A student completely decomposed 3.25 g of sodium azide. Calculate the mass of sodium she obtained. [2]\n\n• (i)She then carefully reacted the sodium obtained with watertoform25.0cm3ofaqueoussodiumhydroxide\n\n2Na(s) + 2H2O(l)→ 2NaOH(aq) +H2(g)\n\nCalculate theconcentration,inmol dm-3,of the aqueoussodiumhydroxide [2]\n\n(Total for Question 11 = 5 marks)"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c0_pdf053_0331", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 2: Atomic Structure and the Periodic Table", "section": "TOPIC2ATOMICSTRUCTURE AND THE PERIODIC TABLE", "content_type": "topic_divider", "book_pages": [40, 40], "pdf_pages": [53, 53], "char_count": 44, "text": "TOPIC2ATOMICSTRUCTURE AND THE PERIODIC TABLE"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c0_pdf053_0332", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 2: Atomic Structure and the Periodic Table", "section": "A ATOMIC STRUCTUREIBTHEPERIODICTABLE", "content_type": "topic_divider", "book_pages": [40, 40], "pdf_pages": [53, 53], "char_count": 1501, "text": "A ATOMIC STRUCTUREIBTHEPERIODICTABLE\n\nChemistryisthe studyof matter and thechanges thatcanbemadetomatter.Matteris anything thathasmass and takes up space.Thisincludes all solids,liquids and gases.The ancient Greeks thought that all matter was madefromfourelements:Air,Earth,FireandWater.Thisideawasgraduallyabandonedasscientistsbeganto experimentandapplylogicalscientificthinkingtotheirobservations.\n\nIn1803,JohnDaltonpresented his atomictheory.Hedeveloped thistheoryfrom theideathatthe atomsof different elements could be distinguished by differences in their weights.Some people still consider that there istruth insomepartsof histheory.However,scientistshavegraduallyrefinedthetheoryintoonethatmore precisely explains the observationswe can make today.Science works by scientists continuing to experiment and make new observations.If scientists cannot explain those observations satisfactorily using the existing theory, then theyhave to change, or even possibly replace,the theory.\n\nAtoms, and the particles they are made from,govern how everything works in the world around us. The continuing study and understandingof atomshas allowed ustoprogresswith developing:new medicinesto newer andbetter materials.\n\nInfact,thereisprobablynot a single areaof modern lifethat isnot affectedby thestudy of atoms.\n\nMATHS SKILLS FOR THIS TOPIC\n\n• Recogniseanduseexpressionsindecimalandordinaryform\n\n• Useratios,fractionsandpercentages\n\n• Use an appropriate number of significant figures\n\n• Findarithmeticmeans"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c0_pdf054_0334", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 2: Atomic Structure and the Periodic Table", "section": "Whatpriorknowledge doI need?", "content_type": "topic_divider", "book_pages": [41, 41], "pdf_pages": [54, 54], "char_count": 531, "text": "Whatpriorknowledge doI need?\n\n• Therelativemassandrelativechargeofaproton, neutronandelectron\n\n• Whereprotons,neutronsandelectronsexist within atoms\n\n• Whatismeantbyatomicnumber,massnumber, isotopes and relative atomic mass(A.)\n\n• Calculationsofrelativeatomicmassfromrelative abundancesof isotopes\n\n• ThePeriodicTableasan arrangementofelements inorderofatomicnumber\n\n• Theelectronicconfigurationsofthefirst20 elementsinthePeriodicTable\n\n• Thenumberofouterelectronsinanatomofa maingroupelementrelatestoitspositioninthe PeriodicTable"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c0_pdf054_0335", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 2: Atomic Structure and the Periodic Table", "section": "Whatwill I study in this topic?", "content_type": "topic_divider", "book_pages": [41, 41], "pdf_pages": [54, 54], "char_count": 358, "text": "Whatwill I study in this topic?\n\n• Howourunderstandingofthestructureofthe atomhasdevelopedovertime\n\n• Howthemassesofatomsandmoleculescanbe determined\n\n• Theevidenceforthe existence ofquantum shells, subshellsandelectronorbitals\n\n• Theelectronicconfigurationsofthefirst36 elementsinthePeriodicTable\n\n• PeriodicityofpropertiesinPeriods2and3of the PeriodicTable"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf055_0336", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [42, 42], "pdf_pages": [55, 55], "char_count": 370, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Knowthestructureofanatomintermsofelectrons,protonsandneutrons.\n\n• Know therelative mass and charge of protons,neutrons and electrons.\n\n• Know the meaning of the terms atomic(proton) number and mass number.\n\n• Beabletousetheatomicnumberandthemassnumbertodeterminethenumberofeachtypeofsubatomicparticle in an atom orion.\n\n• Understandthetermisotope."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf055_0337", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "WHODISCOVEREDELECTRONS,PROTONSANDNEUTRONS?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [42, 42], "pdf_pages": [55, 55], "char_count": 651, "text": "WHODISCOVEREDELECTRONS,PROTONSANDNEUTRONS?\n\nfig A\n\n(a) J.J. Thomson, (b) Ernest Rutherford and (c)James Chadwick.\n\nOur current understanding of the structure of atoms is influenced by the theories put forward by scientists such as J.J. Thomson,Ernest Rutherford and James Chadwick.\n\n• J.J.Thomsondiscovered theelectronin1897.ErnestRutherforddiscovered theproton in1917. JamesChadwickdiscoveredtheneutronin1932.\n\nSTRUCTUREOFANATOM\n\nAlthoughscientistshavediscoveredmanyothersubatomicparticles,chemistryisonlyconcerned withelectrons,protons and neutrons.\n\nWecan summarisethe structure ofthe atominterms ofthese threesubatomicparticles,asshown in table A."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf055_0339", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "STRUCTUREOFANATOM", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [42, 42], "pdf_pages": [55, 55], "char_count": 489, "text": "| PARTICLE   | SYMBOL   |   RELATIVEMASS | RELATIVECHARGE   | POSITIONINTHEATOM                 |\n|------------|----------|----------------|------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| proton     | P        |                | +                | nucleus                           |\n| neutron    | n        |                | 0                | nucleus                           |\n| electron   | e        |           1840 |                  | energylevelssurroundingthenucleus |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf055_0340", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "STRUCTUREOFANATOM", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [42, 42], "pdf_pages": [55, 55], "char_count": 302, "text": "tableAThestructureof theatomintermsofprotons,neutronsandelectrons.\n\nRutherford discovered theproton and he is also credited forfirst suggesting that the atom has avery smallcorecontaining thebulkofthemass ofthe atom.Thiscoreiscalled thenucleus and contains all of the protons and neutrons in that atom."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf055_0341", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [42, 42], "pdf_pages": [55, 55], "char_count": 219, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nNeverstate thatthemassof anelectroniszero.Thisisnottrue,anelectrondoeshave a mass. Youdonotneed toknowtheexactmassesof subatomicparticlesingrams,ortheexactchargesincoulombs. Youneedtoknowtherelativevalues."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf055_0342", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "DID YOU KNOW?", "content_type": "did_you_know", "book_pages": [42, 42], "pdf_pages": [55, 55], "char_count": 316, "text": "DID YOU KNOW?\n\nAllatomsofanyelementhavethesameatomicnumber,whichisdifferentfromtheatomicnumber ofanyotherelement.\n\nThenumberofelectronsinaneutralatomofanelementisequaltothenumberofprotonsinthenucleus of that atom.\n\nTheelectronssurroundthenucleusinwell-definedenergylevelscalledquantumshells.\n\n2.1\n\n2.2\n\n2.3\n\n2.4\n\n2.5"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf056_0343", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "ATOMICNUMBER,MASSNUMBERANDISOTOPES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [43, 43], "pdf_pages": [56, 56], "char_count": 499, "text": "ATOMICNUMBER,MASSNUMBERANDISOTOPES\n\nFig B shows the atomic number,mass number and isotopes of carbon.\n\n• Electron\n\n• OProton\n\n• Neutron\n\nfig BAtomic number,mass number and isotopes of carbon.\n\nTwo isotopes of carbon are 12C and 13C. They have the same atomicnumber,6,and the sameelectronic configuration,2.4,(see\n\nTopic 2A.3),so samples of carbon containing either isotope will have the same chemical properties. However, the mass of 13C is larger than 12C. This is because 13C has one more neutron."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf056_0344", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "ISOTOPES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [43, 43], "pdf_pages": [56, 56], "char_count": 488, "text": "ISOTOPES\n\nIn1919,FrancisAston invented aninstrument called a mass spectrometerHeused it todiscover thatnot all the atoms of anelementhavethesamemass.Itwaseventuallyrealised that thedifferent masseswere caused bythe atoms havingdifferent numbers of neutrons.\n\nThese different atoms are called isotopes.\n\nWe can illustrate this point by considering two isotopes of chlorine: chlorine-35 and chlorine-37.\n\nTable B shows the number of protons,neutrons and electronsin an atom of each isotope."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf056_0345", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "ISOTOPES", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [43, 43], "pdf_pages": [56, 56], "char_count": 447, "text": "tableBIsotopesof chlorine.\n\n| ISOTOPE     | SYMBOL FOR ISOTOPE   |   NUMBEROF PROTONS |   NUMBEROF NEUTRONS |   NUMBEROF ELECTRONS |\n|-------------|----------------------|--------------------|---------------------|----------------------|\n| chlorine-35 | 35C1                 |                 17 |                  18 |                   17 |\n| chlorine-37 | 37C1                 |                 17 |                  20 |                   17 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf056_0346", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "DID YOU KNOW?", "content_type": "did_you_know", "book_pages": [43, 43], "pdf_pages": [56, 56], "char_count": 122, "text": "DID YOU KNOW?\n\nIsotopesof thesameelementhaveidentical chemicalproperties becausetheyhaveidenticalelectronicconfigurations."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf056_0347", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [43, 43], "pdf_pages": [56, 56], "char_count": 415, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1. Use the information in the table below to answer the following questionsabout particles A to F.\n\n• (a)Whichtwoparticlesareisotopesofthesameelement?\n\n• (b)Which twoparticles are positive ions?\n\n• (c)Whichtwoparticlesarenegativeions?\n\n• (d)Which twoparticles have the samemass number?\n\n• 2.Complete the table below to show the numbers of protons, neutrons andelectronsinthese atoms,molecules andions."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf056_0348", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [43, 43], "pdf_pages": [56, 56], "char_count": 623, "text": "|   NUMBEROF PROTONS |   NUMBEROF NEUTRONS | NUMBER OF PARTICLE ELECTRONS   |\n|--------------------|---------------------|--------------------------------|\n|                 12 |                  13 | A 12                           |\n|                 17 |                  18 | B 18                           |\n|                 11 |                  14 | C 10                           |\n|                 12 |                  12 | D 12                           |\n|                 35 |                  44 | E 36                           |\n|                 19 |                  21 | F 18                           |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf056_0349", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [43, 43], "pdf_pages": [56, 56], "char_count": 664, "text": "| SYMBOL FOR ATOM ORION   | NUMBEROF PROTONS   | NUMBER OF NEUTRONS   | NUMBEROF ELECTRONS   |\n|-------------------------|--------------------|----------------------|----------------------|\n| 3H                      |                    |                      |                      |\n| 18 02-                  |                    |                      |                      |\n| 2Mg2+                   |                    |                      |                      |\n| 14NH3                   |                    |                      |                      |\n| 14NH4                   |                    |                      |                      |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf056_0350", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [43, 43], "pdf_pages": [56, 56], "char_count": 236, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\natomicnumber(Z)thenumberofprotonsinthenucleusofanatom massnumberthesumofthenumberofprotonsandthenumberof neutronsinthenucleusof an atom\n\nisotopesatomsofthesameelementthathavethesameatomic numberbutdifferentmassnumber"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf057_0351", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "2MASSSPECTROMETRYAND RELATIVEMASSESOFATOMS ISOTOPESANDMOLECULES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [44, 44], "pdf_pages": [57, 57], "char_count": 63, "text": "2MASSSPECTROMETRYAND RELATIVEMASSESOFATOMS ISOTOPESANDMOLECULES"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf057_0352", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [44, 44], "pdf_pages": [57, 57], "char_count": 518, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Understand thebasicprinciplesof a massspectrometer andbe ableto analyse andinterpretspectrato:deduce theisotopic composition of an element;calculate relative atomicmassfrom the relative abundance of isotopes and vice versa; determine therelativemolecularmassof a molecule,and identifymoleculesinasample.\n\n• Understand thationsinamassspectrometermayhave a 2+charge.\n\n• Be able topredictthemassspectra,includingrelativepeakheights,fordiatomicmoleculesincludingchlorine,giventhe isotopicabundances."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf057_0353", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "RELATIVEATOMICMASSANDRELATIVE ISOTOPICMASS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [44, 44], "pdf_pages": [57, 57], "char_count": 42, "text": "RELATIVEATOMICMASSANDRELATIVE ISOTOPICMASS"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf057_0354", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "RELATIVEATOMICMASS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [44, 44], "pdf_pages": [57, 57], "char_count": 905, "text": "RELATIVEATOMICMASS\n\nChemistsworkinginthe19thcenturyestablished thechemical formulae of some common compounds. They also calculated the massesofatomsfromwhich thesecompoundsareoftenmade In fact, they hadn't measured the actual masses of the atoms but how themassesofatomscomparedwithone another in other wordsrelative atomicmasses.\n\nThe sulfur atom was twice as heavy as the oxygen atom, and the The hydrogen atom was the lightest atom, so its mass was given a value of 1.Themassesofthe atomsof the otherelementswere stated relative to thisvalue.So themassof an atom ofoxygen on this scale was 16,and sulfur was 32.\n\nThe discovery of isotopes complicated the situation. In 1961, scientists decided to adopt an isotope of carbon, carbon-12,as the standard.\n\nRELATIVEISOTOPICMASS\n\nRelative isotopic masses and their percentage abundance in a sample of an element are used to calculate relative atomic masses."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf057_0356", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "WORKED EXAMPLE", "content_type": "worked_example", "book_pages": [44, 44], "pdf_pages": [57, 57], "char_count": 373, "text": "WORKED EXAMPLE\n\nLithium, in its naturally occurring compounds,has two isotopes of relative isotopic masses 6.015 and 7.016. The percentage abundance of each isotope is 7.59 and 92.41 respectively.\n\nThis is how you can calculate the relative atomic mass of lithium. There are two stages.\n\nStage 1:(6.015 x7.59) +(7.016 x92.41)= 694.00241\n\n694.00241 = 6.9400241 100\n\nStage 2:"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf057_0357", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "EXAM HINT", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [44, 44], "pdf_pages": [57, 57], "char_count": 195, "text": "EXAM HINT\n\nTherelativeatomicmasswillalwaysbeanumbersomewhere betweentherelativeisotopicmasses.lfyourcalculatorgivesyoua differentnumber,youhavemadea mistake andshouldgoback and checkyourworkings."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf057_0358", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [44, 44], "pdf_pages": [57, 57], "char_count": 434, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nRememberthat:\n\n• theZnumberisnotthesame astherelativeisotopicmass;the massnumberisalwaysawholenumberbecauseitisthesumof twowholenumbers(thenumberofprotons andthenumberof neutronsin thenucleusofanatom)\n\n• relativeisotopicmassisrelativetothemassofacarbon-12atom; itisnotlikelytobeawholenumber\n\n• relativeisotopicmassesshouldbeusedtocalculaterelative atomicmasses,butsometimesmassnumbersareused tomake the arithmeticeasier."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf057_0359", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "WHATISAMASSSPECTROMETER?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [44, 44], "pdf_pages": [57, 57], "char_count": 411, "text": "WHATISAMASSSPECTROMETER?\n\nA mass spectrometer measures themasses of atoms and molecules\n\nIt produces positive ions that are deflected by a magnetic field according to their mass-to-charge ratio(m/z).It also calculates the relative abundance ofeachpositiveion and displays this as apercentage.\n\nThepositive ions could bepositively charged atoms,positively chargedmoleculesorpositivelychargedfragmentsofmolecules."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf057_0360", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "DID YOU KNOW?", "content_type": "did_you_know", "book_pages": [44, 45], "pdf_pages": [57, 58], "char_count": 468, "text": "DID YOU KNOW?\n\nTherelativeatomicmassofanelementistheweightedmean atomofcarbon-12,whichhasamassof12.\n\nThe relative atomicmass of lithium,to three significantfigures is 6.94.\n\nTherelativeisotopicmassisthemassofanatomofanisotope of the element compared to the mass of an atom of carbon-12, whichhas amassof 12.\n\n2.6\n\n2.7\n\nDealing with fragments of molecules is particularly useful when you are analysing organic compounds. We will discuss this in more detail in Topic 10."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf058_0361", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "HOWDOESAMASSSPECTROMETERWORK?", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [45, 45], "pdf_pages": [58, 58], "char_count": 1546, "text": "HOWDOESAMASSSPECTROMETERWORK?\n\n• figAA mass spectrometer.\n\n• 1.The samplebeing analysed must be in the gaseous state so thatitsparticlescanmove through themachine.Thesampleis injected into the mass spectrometer, where it is vaporised.\n\n• 2. The vapour is bombarded with high energy electrons. These electronscollidewith theatomsormoleculesof thesample Oneor moreelectrons areremovedfrom theatoms or molecules to form positive ions.\n\n• 3.An electricfield causes thepositive ions to accelerate.\n\n• 4.Thepositiveions then enter, and aredeflectedby,a uniform magnetic field. The amount of defection depends on the mass-to-charge (m/z)ratio of theions. Ions with a large massto-charge ratio are deflected less than ions with a small massto-charge ratio. If all the ions have the same charge, usually 1+,the extentof thedeflectionis inverselyproportional to their mass.\n\n• 5. The deflected ions pass through a narrow slit and are collected onametallicplateconnected toan amplifierForagiven strength of magneticfield,only ions with a certain m/z ratio pass through the slit and are detected. For example,an ion with amassof28 anda chargeof1+willbedetected atthesame time as an ion with a mass of 56 and a charge of 2+.Bothions have a m/zratioof28.\n\nThe strength of the magnetic field is then changed to detect positive ions with other m/z ratios.\n\nDETERMININGRELATIVEISOTOPICANDATOMICMASSES\n\nYoucandeterminetheexactvaluesoftherelativemassesof percentage abundanceofeach isotope.\n\nYoucanusethisinformationtocalculatetherelative atomicmass of the element."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf058_0363", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "DETERMININGRELATIVEMOLECULARMASS(M)OFDIATOMIC MOLECULES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [45, 45], "pdf_pages": [58, 58], "char_count": 1042, "text": "DETERMININGRELATIVEMOLECULARMASS(M)OFDIATOMIC MOLECULES\n\nSome elements and compounds contain two or more atoms covalently bonded togetherIf these substances are analysed by massspectrometry,you canobtaintherelativemolecularmassof the element or compound by observing the peaks with the largest m/zratios(assuminga value of z=1).\n\nFig B shows the mass spectrum of chlorine, which exists as diatomic molecules: Cl2.\n\n• figBMass spectrum of chlorine.\n\nTomakecalculationseasier,inthisexampletherelativemolecular and isotopicmasses are quoted aswholenumbers.\n\nThere are two peaks corresponding to isotopic masses of 35 and 37. The approximate relative peak heights for each isotope are 3:1 for chlorine-35: chlorine-37.\n\nThis gives an approximate relative atomic mass of this sample of chlorine of ((3×35)+(1×37))÷4=35.5.\n\nYouwill noticeinthediagram thatthere are threepeaks,which correspond to molecular masses of 70,72 and 74.How can we explain therelativeheightsof thesepeaks?\n\nTable A shows the atomic composition of each molecule of chlorine."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf058_0364", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "DETERMININGRELATIVEMOLECULARMASS(M)OFDIATOMIC MOLECULES", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [45, 45], "pdf_pages": [58, 58], "char_count": 264, "text": "tableAAtomiccompositionofeachmoleculeofchlorine.\n\n|   MASSOFMOLECULE | FORMULAOFMOLECULE   |\n|------------------|---------------------|\n|               70 | 35C135C1            |\n|               72 | 35C137CI            |\n|               74 | 37C137CI            |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf058_0365", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "DETERMININGRELATIVEMOLECULARMASS(M)OFDIATOMIC MOLECULES", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [45, 45], "pdf_pages": [58, 58], "char_count": 140, "text": "tableAAtomiccompositionofeachmoleculeofchlorine.\n\nThe ratio of 35Cl to37Cl in this sample is 3:1. The molecular compositionisshown intableB."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf058_0366", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "DETERMININGRELATIVEMOLECULARMASS(M)OFDIATOMIC MOLECULES", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [45, 45], "pdf_pages": [58, 58], "char_count": 265, "text": "table BMolecular composition of sample.\n\n| FORMULAEOFMOLECULE   |   RATIOOFMOLECULE |\n|----------------------|-------------------|\n| 35C135CI             |                 9 |\n| 35C137CI             |                 6 |\n| 37C137CI             |                 1 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf059_0367", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [46, 46], "pdf_pages": [59, 59], "char_count": 106, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nThepeakheightswill notbe exactly9:6:1becausethe relative isotopicmasses arenot wholenumbers."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf059_0368", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "LEARNING TIP", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [46, 46], "pdf_pages": [59, 59], "char_count": 1124, "text": "LEARNING TIP\n\nMakesureyou alwaysusethe relativeatomicmassesyouare given inanyquestion.For example,thevaluefor magnesiumgiveninthe PeriodicTableonpage306at theendof thisbookis24.3 However,youmayoccasionally beaskedtouseamoreaccurate value,suchas24.305.\n\nLet's explain this.\n\n• ·There is a 3 in 4 chance of selecting a 35Cl atom from a sample of chlorine atoms. This means that the total chance of two 35Cl atoms combining together is × = 6· 9\n\n• There is a 1 in 4 chance of selecting a 37Cl atom. This means that the chance of 37Cl combining with 3\n\n• · The chance of two 37Cl atoms combining together is × = 16 9.6.1 in whole number ratios is 9:6: 1. 16°1616\n\nThis corresponds approximately to thepeak heights of 70,72 and 74.\n\nYou can then determine the relative molecular mass (M,) of chlorine by calculating the weighted mean of thevariousmolecules present:\n\nMr(Cl₂) = (9 × 70) + (6 × 72) + (1 × 74) ÷ 16 = 71\n\nYouwill usuallybeasked tocalculaterelativemolecularmassesbysimply addingtogether the relative atomicmassesof theelementsinthemolecule.\n\nIfweuse this method,the relative molecular mass of chlorine is 2x 35.5=71."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf059_0369", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "DETERMININGRELATIVEMOLECULARMASSOFAPOLYATOMICMOLECULE", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [46, 46], "pdf_pages": [59, 59], "char_count": 813, "text": "DETERMININGRELATIVEMOLECULARMASSOFAPOLYATOMICMOLECULE\n\nIt isquitedifficult touse thedatasuppliedbyamassspectrum ofa compound todetermineits exact relative molecular mass.You need toknow the exactrelative isotopicmasses of all the atomspresent, and therelativecompositionofall thedifferentmolecules.\n\nYou aremorelikelytobeaskedtoworkouttherelativemolecularmassofacompound by consideringwhatiscalledthemolecularionpeak.\n\nYou should be careful when analysing organic compounds. This is because there is always a small percentage of the carbon-13 isotope present in the compound, which can lead to what is referred to carbon-13 becomes significant. The peak is often missing, or insignificant, in molecules of small mass.\n\nThe following examples illustrate this point.\n\nfig DMassspectrum ofethyl benzoate(M,=150)\n\nm/z"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf060_0370", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [47, 47], "pdf_pages": [60, 60], "char_count": 613, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1. Describethedifferencebetweenrelativeatomicmassandrelativeisotopicmass.\n\n• 2. Explainwhy therelative atomicmasses of many elements are notexact whole numbers.\n\n• 3. Calculatetherelativeatomicmassofa sampleof magnesium thathasthefollowingisotopic composition:\n\n• magnesium-24:78.6%\n\n• magnesium-25:10.1%\n\n• magnesium-26:11.3%\n\nGiveyouranswertothreesignificantfigures.\n\n• 4 A sample of copper containstwo isotopes of relative isotopic mass 63.0 and 65.0.If the relative atomicmassofcopperis63.5,calculatetherelativeabundanceofeachisotope.\n\n• 5. Themassspectrumofbrominevapour,Br2,isshowninfigEbelow."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf060_0371", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "fig E", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [47, 47], "pdf_pages": [60, 60], "char_count": 287, "text": "fig E\n\n• (a)What are therelative isotopic masses of the two isotopes present in bromine?\n\n• (b) Identify the particles responsible for the peaks at m/z 158, 160 and 162.\n\n• (c)Deducetherelativeabundanceofthetwoisotopesandexplaintherelativeheightsof the three peaks at m/z158,160 and 162."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf060_0372", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [47, 47], "pdf_pages": [60, 60], "char_count": 343, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nrelative atomic mass(A,)(of anelement) the weighted mean(average) massof an atom of the element comparedtoofthemassofanatomofcarbon-12\n\nrelative isotopicmassthemassof anindividual atom of aparticularisotoperelativetoof themassof an atomofcarbon-12\n\nmolecularionpeakthepeakwith the highestm/zratioin themassspectrum,theMpeak"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf060_0373", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "EXAM HINT", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [47, 47], "pdf_pages": [60, 60], "char_count": 137, "text": "EXAM HINT\n\nInexamquestions,alwaysgive youranswertothenumberof significantfigures askedfor, otherwiseyouwillprobably loseamark.\n\nREASONING"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf061_0374", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "3ATOMICORBITALSAND ELECTRONICCONFIGURATIONS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [48, 48], "pdf_pages": [61, 61], "char_count": 43, "text": "3ATOMICORBITALSAND ELECTRONICCONFIGURATIONS"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf061_0375", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [48, 48], "pdf_pages": [61, 61], "char_count": 549, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Knowthatanorbital isaregionwithin anatomthatcanhold uptotwoelectronswithoppositespins.\n\n• Beabletodescribetheshapesofs andporbitals.\n\n• Be abletopredicttheelectronicconfigurationof the atomsof theelementsfrom hydrogentokryptoninclusive and theirions, using s, p, d notation and electron-in-boxes notation.\n\n• Understand thatelectronic configuration determines thechemical properties of an element.\n\n• Knowthenumberofelectronsthatcanfill thefirstfourquantumshells.\n\n• State thenumberofelectrons thatoccupy s,p and d sub-shells."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf061_0376", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "QUANTUMSHELLS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [48, 48], "pdf_pages": [61, 61], "char_count": 335, "text": "QUANTUMSHELLS\n\nMaxPlanckfirstpresented thequantumtheoryin19oo.We can use this theory to describe the arrangement of electrons around the nuclei of atoms.\n\nAccording to this theory, electrons can only exist in certain welldefined energylevels called quantum shells.\n\nAll electrons ina quantumshell havesimilar,butnotidentical, energies."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf061_0377", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "ELECTRONSINTHEFIRSTFOURQUANTUMSHELLS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [48, 48], "pdf_pages": [61, 61], "char_count": 1013, "text": "ELECTRONSINTHEFIRSTFOURQUANTUMSHELLS\n\nElectrons in thefirst quantum shell have thelowest energyfor that element.Thefirst quantum shell is found in theregion closest to the nucleus. The second quantum shell is in a region outside the first; the third is outside the second, and so on.\n\nEachquantumshell,apartfromthefirst,isfurtherdividedinto sub-shells of slightly different energy levels.\n\n• ·There is only one sub-shell in thefirst quantum shell. This is labelled the 1s sub-shell.\n\n• The second quantum shell has two sub-shells,labelled 2s and 2p. Electrons in the 2p sub-shell have a slightly higher energy level than those in the 2s sub-shell.\n\n• The third quantum shell is divided into three sub-shells, labelled3s,3p and3d.Electronsinthe3psub-shell have slightlyhigher energy than those in 3s,and those in the 3d subshell have slightly higher energy than those in 3p.\n\n• Thefourth quantum shell is divided intofoursub-shells, labelled4s,4p,4d and 4f.Again,theelectron energiesincrease in theorder4s<4p<4d<4f"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf061_0378", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "SORBITALS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [48, 48], "pdf_pages": [61, 61], "char_count": 997, "text": "SORBITALS\n\nEach sub-shell contains orbitals. If you could see a single electron in a hydrogen atom and map its position at regular intervals,thenyou couldconstruct a three-dimensional map of places where the electron is likely tobe found. Most of the time, the electron is located within a fairly easily-defined region of space close to thenucleus.\n\nFig A shows a cross-section through a spherical s orbital.\n\nfig ACross-section through an s orbital.\n\nBecausetheelectronofthehydrogen atomisin anssub-shell,the orbitalisdescribed asaIsorbital.\n\nElectrons in the 2s sub-shell also exist in an s orbital,called the 2s orbital. The 2s orbital has the same shape as the1s orbital but is larger Fig B shows the shape of typical 1s and 2s orbitals.Note that x, y and z are 3D Cartesian axes (i.e. axes at mutual right angles).\n\nThe sizes and shapes of the orbitals mean that there is a 90% probability of finding the electron within their boundaries.\n\n1s\n\n• fig B The shape of typical 1s and 2s orbitals."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf062_0379", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "pORBITALS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [49, 49], "pdf_pages": [62, 62], "char_count": 508, "text": "pORBITALS\n\nThe 2p sub-shell contains three separate p orbitals. These orbitals are more complex and harder to visualise.Theyhave an elongated dumbbell shape and a variablecharge density.The only difference between the three orbitals is their orientation in space, as shown in fig C.\n\nAgain, the size and shape of the orbitals means that there is a 90% probability of finding the electron within their boundaries.The three orbitals are arranged at mutual right angles.\n\nfigCOrientation of the threep orbitals."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf062_0380", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "dORBITALS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [49, 49], "pdf_pages": [62, 62], "char_count": 334, "text": "dORBITALS\n\nThe d orbitals are very complicated. You do not need to know their shapes for IAS or IAL studies. There are five d orbitals in the d sub-shell.\n\nfig DThe five d orbitals.\n\nTherecanbeup totwoelectrons ineachorbital.Using thisinformation,thenumber ofelectrons that canexistineach sub-shellandeachquantum shellisshownintableA."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf062_0381", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "dORBITALS", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [49, 49], "pdf_pages": [62, 62], "char_count": 300, "text": "table AThe number of electrons in each sub-shelland each quantum shell.\n\n|             | NUMBEROFELECTRONS   |\n|-------------|---------------------|\n| s sub-shell | 2 (1 × 2)           |\n| p sub-shell | 6 (3 × 2)           |\n| d sub-shell | 10 (5 × 2)          |\n| f sub-shell | 14 (7 × 2)          |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf062_0382", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "dORBITALS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [49, 49], "pdf_pages": [62, 62], "char_count": 71, "text": "table AThe number of electrons in each sub-shelland each quantum shell."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf062_0383", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "dORBITALS", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [49, 49], "pdf_pages": [62, 62], "char_count": 281, "text": "|                      |   NUMBEROFELECTRONS |\n|----------------------|---------------------|\n| first quantum shell  |                   2 |\n| second quantum shell |                   8 |\n| third quantum shell  |                  18 |\n| fourth quantum shell |                  32 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf062_0384", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "ELECTRONICCONFIGURATIONS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [49, 50], "pdf_pages": [62, 63], "char_count": 226, "text": "ELECTRONICCONFIGURATIONS\n\nThe electronic configuration of an atom of an element is the distribution of electrons among atomic orbitals.\n\nTable B shows the electronic configurationsfor thefirst 36 elements in thePeriodic Table."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u2_c6_pdf063_0385", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 2, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 6, "chapter_title": "2A Atomic structure", "section": "ELECTRONICCONFIGURATIONS", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [50, 50], "pdf_pages": [63, 63], "char_count": 1826, "text": "|   ATOMIC NUMBER | SYMBOL   |   1s |   2s |   2p |   3s |   3p |   3d |   4s |   4p | |-----------------|----------|------|------|------|------|------|------|------|------| |               1 | H        |    1 |      |      |      |      |      |      |      | |               2 | He       |    2 |      |      |      |      |      |      |      | |               3 | Li       |    2 |    1 |      |      |      |      |      |      | |               4 | Be       |    2 |    2 |      |      |      |      |      |      | |               5 | B        |    2 |    2 |    1 |      |      |      |      |      | |               6 | C        |    2 |    2 |    2 |      |      |      |      |      | |               7 | N        |    2 |    2 |    3 |      |      |      |      |      | |               8 | O        |    2 |    2 |    4 |      |      |      |      |      | |               6 | F        |    2 |    2 |    5 |      |      |      |      |      | |              10 | Ne       |    2 |    2 |    6 |      |      |      |      |      | |              11 | Na       |    2 |    2 |    6 |    1 |      |      |      |      | |              12 | Mg       |    2 |    2 |    6 |    2 |      |      |      |      | |              13 | Al       |    2 |    2 |    6 |    2 |    1 |      |      |      | |              14 | Si       |    2 |    2 |    6 |    2 |    2 |      |      |      | |              15 | P        |    2 |    2 |    6 |    2 |    3 |      |      |      | |              16 | S        |    2 |    2 |    