{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c9_pdf090_0535", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 9, "chapter_title": "3B Covalent bonding", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [77, 77], "pdf_pages": [90, 90], "char_count": 681, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1.Suggest why the electronegativity of fluorineis greater than that of chlorine,despite thefact that the nucleusofachlorine atom containsmoreprotons.\n\n• 2.lonic bonding and covalent bonding are two extremes of chemical bonding.Many compounds have bondingthatisintermediateincharacter.\n\n• (a)Giving an example in each case,explain what ismeant by the terms:\n\n• (i) ionicbonding,and(i) covalent bonding.\n\n• (b)Select a compound that has bonding of an intermediate character and explain why it has this type of bonding.\n\n• 3.Place the followingbonds in order of decreasing polarity (i.e.place the most polarfirst).\n\nC—Br C—Cl C—F C—I\n\nExplainhowyou arrived atyouranswer."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c9_pdf090_0536", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 9, "chapter_title": "3B Covalent bonding", "section": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY", "content_type": "subject_vocabulary", "book_pages": [77, 77], "pdf_pages": [90, 90], "char_count": 295, "text": "SUBJECT VOCABULARY\n\nelectronegativitytheabilityof anatomtoattractabondingpairofelectronsinacovalentbond polarcovalentbondatypeofcovalentbondbetweentwoatomswherethebondingelectronsare unequally distributed;becauseof this,one atom carriesa slightnegativecharge and theothera slight positive charge"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c9_pdf091_0537", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 9, "chapter_title": "3B Covalent bonding", "section": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES", "content_type": "learning_objectives", "book_pages": [78, 78], "pdf_pages": [91, 91], "char_count": 256, "text": "LEARNING OBJECTIVES\n\n• Understandwhat ismeantby theterm discrete(simple) molecule.\n\n• Draw dot-and-cross diagrams to show electrons in discrete molecules with single, double and triple bonds\n\n• Drawdisplayedformulaetorepresentthebondingindiscretemolecules."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c9_pdf091_0538", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 9, "chapter_title": "3B Covalent bonding", "section": "DID YOU KNOW?", "content_type": "did_you_know", "book_pages": [78, 78], "pdf_pages": [91, 91], "char_count": 508, "text": "DID YOU KNOW?\n\nMoleculesare commonin organic substances(and therefore biochemistry).They alsomakeupmostofthe oceansand the atmosphere.\n\nHowever,ionic crystals(salts) andgiantcovalentcrystals (network solids) are often madeupofrepeatingunit cellsthatextendeitherina plane (such asingraphene) or three-dimensionally (suchasindiamond or sodium chloride).These substances arenot composed ofmolecules.Solid metals are also notcomposed of molecules.\n\nfigADot-and-crossdiagram for hydrogen with overlapping circles."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c9_pdf091_0539", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 9, "chapter_title": "3B Covalent bonding", "section": "DOT-AND-CROSSDIAGRAMS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [78, 78], "pdf_pages": [91, 91], "char_count": 228, "text": "DOT-AND-CROSSDIAGRAMS\n\nCovalent and polar covalent bonding in discrete molecules can be shown by dot-and-cross diagrams.\n\nFig A shows the example of hydrogen, H2.\n\nFurther examples of dot-and-cross diagrams are shown in table A."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c9_pdf091_0540", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 9, "chapter_title": "3B Covalent bonding", "section": "DOT-AND-CROSSDIAGRAMS", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [78, 78], "pdf_pages": [91, 91], "char_count": 448, "text": "tableADot-and-cross diagramsforwater,ammonia and methane.\n\n| SUBSTANCE    | DOT-AND-CROSSDIAGRAM                                       |\n|--------------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Water, HO    | HOH                                                        |\n| Ammonia, NH3 | H H*N*H nucleus of hydrogen atom (electronegativity 2.1) H |\n| Methane,CH4  | H HCH                                                      |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c9_pdf091_0541", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 9, "chapter_title": "3B Covalent bonding", "section": "DOT-AND-CROSSDIAGRAMS", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [78, 78], "pdf_pages": [91, 91], "char_count": 57, "text": "tableADot-and-cross diagramsforwater,ammonia and methane."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c9_pdf091_0542", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 9, "chapter_title": "3B Covalent bonding", "section": "EXAM HINT", "content_type": "exam_hint", "book_pages": [78, 78], "pdf_pages": [91, 91], "char_count": 118, "text": "EXAM HINT\n\nWhen drawingamoleculesuchaschloromethane,donotforgettoshowallof thenon-bondingelectrons on thechlorineatom."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c9_pdf092_0543", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 9, "chapter_title": "3B Covalent bonding", "section": "THEOCTETRULE", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [79, 79], "pdf_pages": [92, 92], "char_count": 429, "text": "THEOCTETRULE\n\nYou mightread that in order toform a stable compound,theoutershellofeach atom musthave the samenumber ofelectrons asthe outershellof a noblegas.Inmostcases thiswill beeightelectrons. This has led to a rule that is oftenreferred toas the'octet rule'.\n\nThis is not always true, as you can see from the examples in table B. In each case, the outer shell of the central atom ofthemoleculedoes not containeightelectrons."}
