{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c12_pdf106_0614", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 12, "chapter_title": "3E Solid lattices", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [93, 93], "pdf_pages": [106, 106], "char_count": 184, "text": "CHECKPOINT\n\n• 1.The table belowgives some properties of four substances:A,B,C and D.\n\nAnalysethedata and thendecidewhat typeof bondingandstructureislikelytobepresent in each substance."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c12_pdf106_0615", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 12, "chapter_title": "3E Solid lattices", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [93, 93], "pdf_pages": [106, 106], "char_count": 863, "text": "|      | MELTING TEMPERATURE/°C   | ABILITYTOCONDUCT ELECTRICITYWHENSOLID   | ABILITYTOCONDUCT ELECTRICITYWHENMOLTEN   | SOLUBILITYIN WATER   |\n|------|--------------------------|-----------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n| 1083 | good                     |                                         | insoluble                                |                      |\n|  119 | poor                     | poor                                    | insoluble                                |                      |\n| 2230 | poor                     | poor                                    |                                          | insoluble            |\n|  801 | poor                     |                                         |                                          | soluble              |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c12_pdf106_0616", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": 12, "chapter_title": "3E Solid lattices", "section": "CHECKPOINT", "content_type": "checkpoint", "book_pages": [93, 93], "pdf_pages": [106, 106], "char_count": 553, "text": "• 2.Each of the substances listed belowhas at least one property that is unusual forits type of bonding and properties.Suggesta possibleidentityforeachsubstance.\n\n• (a)Substance P.\n\nMeltingtemperature813°C;goodconductorofelectricitywhensolid andwhenmolten;reactswith watertoformammoniagasandanalkalinesolution.\n\n• (b)Substance Q.\n\nMelting temperature1414°C;semi-conductorof electricitywhen solid;insolubleinwater.\n\n• (c)Substance R.\n\nMeltingtemperature98°C;goodconductorofelectricitywhensolid;reactswithwatertoform hydrogen gas and an alkaline solution."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c0_pdf107_0617", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 3: Bonding and Structure", "section": "ACHEMICALINTERVENTION", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [94, 94], "pdf_pages": [107, 107], "char_count": 127, "text": "ACHEMICALINTERVENTION\n\nIn the following story,an attempt to ensure ideal skiing conditions led to wider environmental concerns."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c0_pdf107_0618", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 3: Bonding and Structure", "section": "AMMONIUMNITRATEGOESONTHEPISTE", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [94, 94], "pdf_pages": [107, 107], "char_count": 1592, "text": "AMMONIUMNITRATEGOESONTHEPISTE\n\nSwiss winter sports event organisers have caused an environmental stirbyusingchemicalfertiliserstomaintaintheirpreciousslopes. Itemergedthatupto1.5tonnesof ammoniumnitratewasusedto prepare and protect the piste for the Lauberhorn downhill ski race in Switzerland.Environmental researchers are now investigating the extent andeffectsof thispractice.\n\nAs with any salt, dissolving ammonium nitrate involves breaking it intoits constituent ammonium and nitrate ions,which takes inenergyfromitssurroundings.Theformationof newbonds between these ions and surrounding water molecules then releases energy.Butsince ammonium andnitrateionsarerelatively large,thewatermoleculeshaverelativelyweakinteractionswith theirdiffusecharges.Sowithlittlethermodynamicpaybackduring thisbondformation,theimmediateeffectof addingammonium nitratetoslushysnowis tocool it down.\n\nChristianRixenfrom theSwissFederalResearchInstitutereported thatammoniumnitrateisusedforraceswherewetsnowcanslow skiersdown.Hisresearchhighlightedsome of theenvironmental risks of this practice.\n\nThisis avery strongfertilizer.Athigh elevation,youhave alpine meadowswithvegetation that lives within nutrient-poor soil. If this ishighlyfertilized it canhave negative effects,，saidRixen. We have already seen that a single application of ammonium nitrate can cause a reduction in biodiversity,'he said.\n\nThepracticehas alsoraisedquestions aboutthecontamination of waterways.'It could even be a problem for organic farmers in the area,who are not allowed to use any chemicals at all on their land, added Rixen."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c0_pdf107_0619", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 3: Bonding and Structure", "section": "AMMONIUMNITRATEGOESONTHEPISTE", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [94, 95], "pdf_pages": [107, 108], "char_count": 1126, "text": "He is nowworking closely with the SwissFederal Officefor the Environment andsaysthatmoreinformationisneededabouthow widespread thispracticeis.Agricultural use of chemical fertilisers is already subject to detailedguidelines,includingregulations barring its use at high altitudes and on snow-coveredfields.\n\nHowever,DanielHartmannfromtheSwissFederalOfficeforthe Environmentreported thatnospecificrulesexistcontrollingthe use of fertilizers on ski slopes.\n\nThere are many rules to control its agricultural use because farming is such a chemically-intensive practice,' he explained. Weneedtofindoutmore abouthowwidelythesechemicalsare used onski slopes and,if necessary,reviewthepolicy and define rules tobeput inplace toprotect the environment.'\n\nFrom an article in ChemistryWorld,Cooling chemical fuels snowy spat byVictoria Gill,24 January 2007.Reproduced by permission of The Royal Society of Chemistry.\n\nSCIENCE COMMUNICATION\n\n• 1. Imagine you are writing an article for an organic farming magazine. How could you re-write this story to emphasise the environmental issues without changing the factual content of the article?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c0_pdf108_0621", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 3: Bonding and Structure", "section": "CHEMISTRY IN DETAIL", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [95, 95], "pdf_pages": [108, 108], "char_count": 877, "text": "CHEMISTRY IN DETAIL\n\n• 2. (a) Write down the chemical formula of ammonium nitrate and calculate the percentage by mass ofnitrogeninthecompound.\n\n• (b)Ammonium nitrateisanioniccompound.Describea test thatyou couldcarry outin thelab toshow this and state the expectedresults.\n\n• (c)Describe the bonding in the NHt ion and explain its shape.\n\n• 3. Ammonium nitrate decomposes on heating to produce nitrogen, oxygen and water.\n\n• (a)Write abalanced chemical equationfor this decomposition.\n\n• (b)Usingyour answer to part(a),calculate thevolume ofoxygen(measured atr.t.p) that would be produced if one tonne of ammonium nitrate decomposed. (Assume 1 mole of any gas occupies 24 dm3 at r.t.p.)\n\n• (c)Using information from the article and your answers to the questions above suggest which twohazard symbolsyouwould expect tofind on a container of ammonium nitrate.Explainyourchoices."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c0_pdf108_0622", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 3: Bonding and Structure", "section": "ACTIVITY", "content_type": "thinking_bigger", "book_pages": [95, 95], "pdf_pages": [108, 108], "char_count": 596, "text": "ACTIVITY\n\nThe article raises the issue of a possible problem for organic farmers in the area'. Carry out an internetresearchinvestigationintothefollowingquestions:\n\n• i)What is meant by'organic farming'?\n\n• ii)What are the possible advantages and disadvantages of this approach to farming?\n\n• farming.Your teacher may allocate you the side of the argument that you may not feel you agreewithsoyouwill needtopreparecarefully\n\nINTELLECTUALINTEREST ANDCURIOSITY, ADAPTABILITY\n\nINTERPRETATIONNOTE\n\nBeconstructivelycritical aboutthenatureofthe sourcematerialontheweb Whatconstitutesareliable resource?"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c0_pdf109_0624", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 3: Bonding and Structure", "section": "3 EXAMPRACTICE", "content_type": "chapter_questions", "book_pages": [96, 96], "pdf_pages": [109, 109], "char_count": 1611, "text": "3 EXAMPRACTICE\n\n• 1Which of thefollowing statements about the properties associatedwithionicandcovalentcompoundsiscorrect?\n\n• 6Whichofthefollowingmoleculesdoesnothave apermanent dipole?\n\n• A An ionic compound cannot undergo electrolysis.\n\nA CF4\n\nB CHF3\n\n• C CH2F2\n\nD CHF\n\n[1]\n\n• B The only covalently bonded substances with high melting present.\n\n• C A compound cannot contain both ionic bonding and covalent bonding.\n\n• DIonic compounds differ from metals because they do not conduct electricityin the solid state. [1]\n\n(Total for Question 1 =1 mark)\n\n• 2Which is the best explanation for the ability of graphite to act as a solid lubricant?\n\n• AIthasdelocalisedelectrons thatare abletoflowparallelto the layers.\n\n• B The covalent bonds within the layers are strong.\n\n• C Gas molecules are trapped between the layers.\n\n• DTheforces of attractionbetween thelayers are very weak.(1)\n\n(TotalforQuestion 2=1mark)\n\n• 3Which of the following molecules contains sixbonding electrons?\n\nA CH4\n\nB CO2\n\nCHS\n\nD NCl3\n\n[1]\n\n(Total for Question 3 = 1 mark)\n\n• 4Asolid melts just above100°C.It doesnot conduct electricity evenwhenmolten.\n\n• Which of the following structures is the compound most likely to have?\n\n• A giant ionic\n\n• B giant covalent\n\n• C simple molecular\n\n• D giant metallic\n\n[1] (Total for Question 4=1mark)\n\n• 5The speciesAr,K+and Ca²+are isoelectronic.\n\nIn what order dotheirradii increase?\n\nsmallest largest\n\nA\n\nAr Ca2+\n\nK+\n\nB Ar K+\n\nCa2+\n\nC\n\nCa2+\n\nAr K+\n\nD\n\nCa2+\n\nK+\n\nAr\n\n[1]\n\n(Total for Question 5 = 1 mark)\n\n(Total for Question 6 = 1 mark)\n\n• 7Lithium, magnesium and sodium all exhibit metallic bonding."}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c0_pdf109_0625", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 3: Bonding and Structure", "section": "3 EXAMPRACTICE", "content_type": "chapter_questions", "book_pages": [96, 96], "pdf_pages": [109, 109], "char_count": 531, "text": "• (a)State what is meant by metallic bonding. [2]\n\n• (b) State how a metal can conduct electricity.\n\n[1]\n\n• (c)Explain why magnesium is a better conductor of electricity than sodium. [2]\n\n• (d)Predict whether or not sodium is a better electrical conductorthanlithium.Justifyyour answer\n\n(TotalforQuestion 7=7marks)\n\n[2]\n\n• 8The table shows some of thepropertiesoffour substances,\n\nA,B, C and D.\n\nState the type of bonding and structure that is likely to be present in each of the substances.\n\nIn each case justify your answer.\n\n[8]"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c0_pdf109_0627", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 3: Bonding and Structure", "section": "A,B, C and D.", "content_type": "table_data", "book_pages": [96, 96], "pdf_pages": [109, 109], "char_count": 929, "text": "| Substance   | Solubility in water   | Electrical conductivity - of solid   | Electrical conductivity - in aqueous solution   |   Melting temperature/C |\n|-------------|-----------------------|--------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|-------------------------|\n| A           | insoluble             | poor                                 |                                                 |                    1610 |\n| B           | soluble               | poor                                 | good                                            |                     801 |\n| C           | insoluble             | poog                                 | 一                                               |                    1083 |\n| D           | soluble               | poor                                 | fair                                            |                     -78 |"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c0_pdf109_0628", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 3: Bonding and Structure", "section": "A,B, C and D.", "content_type": "chapter_questions", "book_pages": [96, 97], "pdf_pages": [109, 110], "char_count": 1264, "text": "(Total for Question 8=8marks)\n\n• 9The electronpair repulsion(EPR) theory can be used topredict the shape of simplemolecules and ions.\n\n• (a) State the main assumptions of the EPR theory. [3]\n\n• (b)Draw a dot-and-cross diagram for each of the following molecules/ions.\n\n• (i)BF3 (ii) NH3 (ii) NH4+ (iv) SF6 [4]\n\n• (c)Predict the shape and bond angles in each of the molecule/ions in part (b).\n\n[5]\n\n(Total for Question 9 = 12 marks)\n\n• 10Nitrogen and carbon monoxide areboth gases consisting of diatomic molecules. Both gases are colourless, odourless and tasteless,butunlike nitrogen,carbonmonoxideis extremely toxic.\n\n• (a)The dot-and-cross diagram for carbonmonoxide, showing only the outer electrons, is:\n\n• C0: ：\n\n• Copy the diagram and addlabels to identify:\n\n• (i)a lone pair of electrons\n\n• (i)a covalent bondingpair of electrons and\n\n• (i) a dative covalent bonding pair of electrons.\n\n[3]\n\n• (b)The nitrogen and carbon monoxide molecules are isoelectronic.\n\n• (i)State what is meant by isoelectronic.\n\n[1]\n\n• (i) The HCN molecule is also isoelectronic with N2 and Co.\n\n• Draw a dot-and-cross diagram for HCN, showing only the outershellelectrons.\n\n[2]\n\n• (i) Suggest why nitrogen is much less reactive than either carbon monoxide or hydrogen cyanide.\n\n[1]"}
{"id": "ych1_v1_u3_c0_pdf110_0629", "book_title": "Pearson Edexcel International AS/A Level Chemistry Student Book 1", "author": "Cliff Curtis, Jason Murgatroyd, David Scott", "unit_no": 3, "unit_name": "Unit 1: Structure, Bonding and Introduction to Organic Chemistry", "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Topic 3: Bonding and Structure", "section": "（TotalforQuestion10=7marks)", "content_type": "chapter_questions", "book_pages": [97, 97], "pdf_pages": [110, 110], "char_count": 1297, "text": "（TotalforQuestion10=7marks)\n\n• 11Sodium chloride is an ionic compound containing sodium ions (Nat) and chloride ions (Cl-).\n\n• It has a fairly high melting temperature, is soluble in water and is apoorconductorofelectricitywhensolid,butgoodwhen molten.\n\n• (a)Draw dot-and-cross diagrams to show the changes in arrangementofelectronsthattakeplacewhensodium (Na) reacts with chlorine(Cl2)to form sodium chloride. Showonly theouterelectronsineachcase.\n\n[4]\n\n• (b)Complete the diagram to show the structure of sodium chloride using the key provided. [2]\n\n• (c)Explain why sodium chloride\n\n• (i)has a fairly high melting temperature [3]\n\n• (i)issoluble in water\n\n[3]\n\n• (ii) is a poor conductor of electricity when solid, but a good conductor when molten. [2]\n\n(Total for Question 11 =14 marks)\n\n• 12Thisquestion is about four simplemolecular compounds: water, ammonia,methane and boron trichloride.\n\n• (a)Complete the table to show the number of bonding pairs and non-bonding (lone) pairs of electrons in one molecule ofeachcompound. [4]\n\n• (b)The ammonia molecule and theborontrichloride moleculeboth containpolarbonds.\n\n• (i)State what is meant by a polar bond, and explain how the polarity arises. [3]\n\n• (i) Explain why the ammonia molecule is polar, but the boron trichloride molecule is not. [3]"}
